00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:21.206
The hottest of all was 2016.


00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:25.410
This graph shows
average temperatures


00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:28.579
from 1951 through 1980.


00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.715
The white are the normal days,


00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.652
the blue are
the cooler-than-average days,


00:07:34.688 --> 00:07:37.489
and the red are
warmer-than-average days.


00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:41.726
And in the 1980s,
the entire curve


00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:43.861
shifted to the warm side.


00:07:43.897 --> 00:07:45.730
And we saw, for the first time,


00:07:45.765 --> 00:07:48.833
the appearance of
a statistically significant


00:07:48.869 --> 00:07:52.470
number of extremely
hot days in the lower right.


00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:56.140
In the 1990s,
the curve shifted further.


00:07:56.176 --> 00:07:57.975
And in the last ten years,


00:07:58.011 --> 00:08:02.647
the extremely hot days
have become more numerous


00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:05.149
than the
cooler-than-average days.


00:08:05.185 --> 00:08:08.753
We still have cool days.
We still have cold days.


00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:11.455
But these extremely hot days


00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:14.926
are becoming much more numerous.


