00:18:18.665 --> 00:18:21.198
And, of course,
the low-lying islands,


00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:26.237
the Maldives have an enormous
amount at risk.


00:18:27.206 --> 00:18:29.707
Kiribati has
already purchased land


00:18:29.742 --> 00:18:32.310
to move its entire population.


00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:38.215
And again, this is from
the city we're in right now.


00:18:38.251 --> 00:18:40.784
I mean, I just wonder
how the governor


00:18:40.819 --> 00:18:42.453
sloshes through this and says,


00:18:42.489 --> 00:18:44.856
"I don't notice anything.
Do you notice anything?"


00:18:49.528 --> 00:18:51.328
<i>So, Florida...</i>


00:18:51.363 --> 00:18:55.099
- Florida.
- ...is a challenge, and so...


00:18:55.134 --> 00:18:57.200
- I can confirm that.
- Okay.


00:18:57.236 --> 00:18:59.336
So, that's the big question
we have.


00:18:59.372 --> 00:19:00.571
I cover the climate,


00:19:00.606 --> 00:19:02.339
environmental stuff
all the time,


00:19:02.374 --> 00:19:04.008
and you have a state
with a governor


00:19:04.043 --> 00:19:06.010
who wouldn't even
meet with scientists


00:19:06.045 --> 00:19:08.146
- to talk about climate change.
- Yeah.


00:19:09.047 --> 00:19:11.281
How do you move forward on it?


00:19:11.316 --> 00:19:13.918
In order to address
the environmental crisis,


00:19:13.953 --> 00:19:16.420
we are gonna have to
spend some time


00:19:16.456 --> 00:19:18.789
fixing the democracy crisis.


00:19:18.825 --> 00:19:22.259
Because big money
has so much influence now,


00:19:22.294 --> 00:19:24.896
our democracy has been hacked.


00:19:24.931 --> 00:19:29.266
Large contributors
call the shots.


00:19:29.302 --> 00:19:31.635
Have you ever thought about
running for office again?


00:19:31.671 --> 00:19:35.238
I've used this line before,
so forgive me,


00:19:35.274 --> 00:19:38.308
but I am
a recovering politician.


00:19:38.344 --> 00:19:40.377
And the longer I go
without a relapse,


00:19:40.412 --> 00:19:42.546
the less likely one becomes.


00:19:46.085 --> 00:19:47.484
<i>So, I just wanted to say,</i>


00:19:47.519 --> 00:19:49.464
<i>we'll probably do, sort of,
three buckets of stuff.</i>


00:19:49.488 --> 00:19:50.621
- All right.
- So, there's...


00:19:50.656 --> 00:19:51.800
- Is one of them climate?
- Yes, climate.


00:19:51.824 --> 00:19:53.824
Yes. One of them is climate,


00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.594
one of them is sort of
broadly like how politics


00:19:56.629 --> 00:19:58.061
are different now
than they were,


00:19:58.096 --> 00:19:59.730
- say, 15 years ago.
- Sure.


00:19:59.765 --> 00:20:01.298
Citizens United...


00:20:01.333 --> 00:20:03.200
I'm interested to hear
your thoughts on that.


00:20:03.236 --> 00:20:05.469
- Okay, yeah.
- And then some 2016 stuff.


00:20:05.505 --> 00:20:07.271
Okay. But I'm not gonna...


00:20:07.306 --> 00:20:08.416
You can decline.
I know you're not gonna...


00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:09.673
I'm not gonna commit news.


00:20:09.708 --> 00:20:12.343
Yes, I know.
But I'll try to get you to.


00:20:12.378 --> 00:20:14.211
Okay.


00:20:14.246 --> 00:20:15.357
But we will talk about climate?


00:20:15.381 --> 00:20:16.647
Yeah, yeah, yeah!


00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:19.350
I got to feed the beast,
Mr. Vice President.


00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:23.821
<i>Sometimes it seems to me that</i>


00:20:23.856 --> 00:20:28.025
<i>the climate crisis is simply
not getting the kind of</i>


00:20:28.061 --> 00:20:30.227
<i>coverage in the media
that it should.</i>


00:20:30.262 --> 00:20:32.563
<i>You have a
Republican Party right now...</i>


00:20:32.598 --> 00:20:34.464
<i>Historically large field, right?</i>


00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.099
<i>There's no one on climate...</i>


00:20:36.135 --> 00:20:37.368
<i>Yeah, it's odd, isn't it?</i>


00:20:37.403 --> 00:20:38.936
<i>...in the entire lineup.</i>


00:20:38.972 --> 00:20:42.940
Since when did the United States


00:20:42.975 --> 00:20:46.610
abandon its traditional
world leadership role?


00:20:46.645 --> 00:20:49.980
Especially at a time when,
just this past week,


00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:51.915
the President of China says,


00:20:51.951 --> 00:20:55.352
"Okay, we're going to adopt
a cap and trade program,"


00:20:55.387 --> 00:20:58.556
"and we're reducing
our CO2 emissions,"


00:20:58.591 --> 00:21:00.524
"and we want to create jobs"


00:21:00.559 --> 00:21:02.993
"in solar and wind
and efficiency."


00:21:03.029 --> 00:21:04.829
<i>This is the most serious</i>


00:21:04.864 --> 00:21:07.164
<i>global challenge
we've ever faced.</i>


00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.532
<i>No other country
can play the role</i>


00:21:08.568 --> 00:21:10.368
<i>that the U.S. can play.</i>


00:21:13.539 --> 00:21:14.816
<i>Do you think that we're reaching</i>


00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.108
<i>that tipping point
to the point that</i>


00:21:17.143 --> 00:21:19.009
it's not going to be
any more denial?


00:21:19.045 --> 00:21:21.345
We are at a turning point.


00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:24.448
And we can successfully
reach an agreement


00:21:24.484 --> 00:21:27.518
in this big global negotiation
in Paris


00:21:27.553 --> 00:21:29.753
at the end of November


00:21:29.789 --> 00:21:33.357
to have a real meaningful turn
in the right direction.


00:21:33.393 --> 00:21:35.659
At what moment did you decide
that you wanted to leave


00:21:35.695 --> 00:21:40.497
politics aside and actually
move into this, uh,


00:21:40.533 --> 00:21:42.566
new career that you have?


00:21:42.601 --> 00:21:45.369
Well, to be honest,
that decision was one made


00:21:45.404 --> 00:21:47.638
by the Supreme Court
of the United States.


00:21:48.541 --> 00:21:53.443
I enjoyed politics,
but this is a mission


00:21:53.479 --> 00:21:56.746
that I have dedicated myself to.


00:21:56.782 --> 00:22:01.285
And there's a hunger
for information


00:22:01.321 --> 00:22:03.420
about what's happening,
why it's happening,


00:22:03.455 --> 00:22:05.456
and how we can fix it.


00:22:15.401 --> 00:22:17.234
<i>I usually start
with a black screen.</i>


00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:18.735
<i>And, trust me,</i>


00:22:18.771 --> 00:22:21.972
after only
two or three times through,


00:22:22.008 --> 00:22:26.443
you will associate your own
way of telling each story


00:22:26.478 --> 00:22:29.546
with the picture,
and it'll come so easily.


00:22:29.581 --> 00:22:31.915
The way the memory works...


00:22:31.951 --> 00:22:35.085
<i>Ten years ago,
I made the decision</i>


00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.420
<i>to launch a training program,</i>


00:22:37.456 --> 00:22:41.625
<i>so that anyone
who wanted to learn</i>


00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:45.696
<i>the skills to communicate
to thousands of others</i>


00:22:45.731 --> 00:22:48.332
could come and get trained.


00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:49.967
<i>...'cause really,
all we're trying to do</i>


00:22:50.002 --> 00:22:51.301
<i>is get the first stage going,</i>


00:22:51.336 --> 00:22:54.137
<i>which is just waking people up.</i>


00:22:54.173 --> 00:22:56.873
<i>There were only 50
of them in the beginning.</i>


00:22:56.909 --> 00:22:59.610
<i>But I look back
on that first training</i>


00:23:00.379 --> 00:23:01.678
and it makes me smile,


00:23:01.714 --> 00:23:04.548
because they were real pioneers,


00:23:04.584 --> 00:23:06.717
in my way of thinking about it.


00:23:06.752 --> 00:23:09.386
This is the first picture
that any of us ever saw


00:23:09.421 --> 00:23:11.255
of the Earth from space.


00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:14.358
It was taken
on Christmas Eve, 1968,


00:23:14.393 --> 00:23:16.694
during the Apollo 8 mission.


00:23:16.729 --> 00:23:19.863
And this was the first time
that human beings


00:23:19.898 --> 00:23:23.667
left near-Earth orbit
and went far enough into space


00:23:23.702 --> 00:23:27.938
to see the planet whole,
floating in the void.


00:23:32.745 --> 00:23:36.480
<i>And I've always
started my slideshows</i>


00:23:36.515 --> 00:23:39.183
<i>with those pictures.</i>


00:23:40.319 --> 00:23:45.489
<i>When people can see
the Earth from space,</i>


00:23:45.524 --> 00:23:48.992
they naturally find it easier
to feel a connection


00:23:49.028 --> 00:23:51.896
to our shared home.


00:23:55.534 --> 00:23:58.535
<i>And the last image
from the Apollo program,</i>


00:23:58.571 --> 00:24:00.004
The Blue Marble,


00:24:00.039 --> 00:24:04.908
<i>the one picture of the entire
Earth fully illuminated,</i>


00:24:04.943 --> 00:24:10.848
<i>completely changed the way
people think about the planet.</i>


00:24:10.883 --> 00:24:14.051
<i>It energized the modern
environmental movement.</i>


00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.589
I put that picture
on my office wall


00:24:18.624 --> 00:24:21.224
in the West Wing
of the White House,


00:24:21.259 --> 00:24:23.060
and I looked at it every day.


00:24:24.729 --> 00:24:27.064
<i>I called up NASA and I said,</i>


00:24:27.099 --> 00:24:29.766
<i>"Hey, I've been looking
at this same picture here,"</i>


00:24:29.801 --> 00:24:33.637
<i>"and I'm just wondering
if there's another one."</i>


00:24:33.673 --> 00:24:37.974
<i>I thought, what if we could
have images on a daily basis?</i>


00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:42.446
<i>Might that help to build
the commitment people have</i>


00:24:42.481 --> 00:24:44.949
<i>for saving the climate balance?</i>


00:24:45.785 --> 00:24:47.350
And that's when I learned


00:24:47.386 --> 00:24:49.253
there's really not another one.


00:24:50.722 --> 00:24:56.093
<i>That's what led to the idea
of the DSCOVR satellite.</i>


00:24:56.128 --> 00:24:58.962
<i>Not only for these pictures,
but because of</i>


00:24:58.998 --> 00:25:02.565
<i>the amazing scientific
data gathering that you can do</i>


00:25:02.601 --> 00:25:04.835
<i>from that special point
in space.</i>


00:25:06.872 --> 00:25:08.872
There was opposition
in the Congress.


00:25:08.908 --> 00:25:11.041
I was about to run
for president,


00:25:11.076 --> 00:25:13.644
and that may have had
something to do with it.


00:25:15.414 --> 00:25:18.648
<i>But once I finally
got it approved,</i>


00:25:18.684 --> 00:25:21.151
<i>other instruments
started being added to it.</i>


00:25:21.821 --> 00:25:24.088
<i>And one was the crucial</i>


00:25:24.123 --> 00:25:28.592
<i>early-warning device
for solar storms</i>


00:25:28.627 --> 00:25:32.062
that threaten electric
utility grids and pipelines.


00:25:34.033 --> 00:25:37.034
<i>And NASA built the satellite,
gave it a launch date.</i>


00:25:38.671 --> 00:25:41.771
<i>And then after
the Supreme Court decision</i>


00:25:41.807 --> 00:25:45.676
<i>and the inauguration
of Bush and Cheney,</i>


00:25:45.711 --> 00:25:48.312
they canceled
the satellite launch.


00:25:52.051 --> 00:25:54.818
<i>Please stand for the invocation.</i>


00:25:55.454 --> 00:25:56.820
<i>Reverend Graham.</i>


00:25:58.624 --> 00:26:01.692
<i>The new administration,</i>


00:26:01.727 --> 00:26:04.894
<i>they didn't really realize
they were also canceling</i>


00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:08.665
<i>this solar storm
early-warning system.</i>


00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.336
<i>And the businesses
that depend on it</i>


00:26:12.371 --> 00:26:15.739
<i>started making a lot of noise.</i>


00:26:16.775 --> 00:26:20.077
<i>And they proposed
to resolve that quandary</i>


00:26:20.112 --> 00:26:25.082
by taking all of
the climate instruments


00:26:25.117 --> 00:26:28.185
and the camera
off of the satellite,


00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:31.722
replacing them with
the equivalent of sandbags


00:26:31.757 --> 00:26:34.091
and only leaving
the one instrument


00:26:34.126 --> 00:26:38.762
that these powerful industries
wanted to be put into orbit.


00:26:39.565 --> 00:26:44.768
I thought,
"Wow, that is extremism."


00:26:46.705 --> 00:26:48.605
<i>By the end of it,</i>


00:26:48.641 --> 00:26:52.242
<i>this satellite was
put in storage.</i>


00:26:56.615 --> 00:26:58.649
<i>We had a real opportunity</i>


00:26:58.684 --> 00:27:01.818
<i>to start building
enough public support</i>


00:27:01.854 --> 00:27:05.055
<i>to really get on track
to solving the climate crisis.</i>


00:27:06.258 --> 00:27:08.625
But we lost that opportunity.


00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:12.830
And now, we cannot afford
to lose it again.


00:27:28.046 --> 00:27:29.246
Okay, Bo.


00:27:40.292 --> 00:27:41.691
<i>This house was my home</i>


00:27:41.727 --> 00:27:43.527
<i>when I was growing up as a boy.</i>


00:27:43.562 --> 00:27:46.330
<i>My mother and father built it.</i>


00:27:48.167 --> 00:27:50.267
LBJ came to this farm.


00:27:50.302 --> 00:27:54.104
He fell out with my dad
when my dad became one of


00:27:54.139 --> 00:27:57.340
the leading opponents
of the Vietnam War,


00:27:57.375 --> 00:28:02.045
but they served in the Senate
together a long time.


00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:05.382
Here's a picture of my dad
doing<i> Meet the Press,</i> and...


00:28:05.417 --> 00:28:08.919
Then, many years later,
me doing<i> Meet the Press.</i>


00:28:10.489 --> 00:28:11.855
Here's a, uh,


00:28:13.959 --> 00:28:17.594
handwritten note that
my second-oldest daughter,


00:28:17.629 --> 00:28:21.732
Kristin, wrote back in 1987,


00:28:21.767 --> 00:28:24.067
when we had
a family meeting to go over


00:28:24.102 --> 00:28:26.937
whether or not
I should run for President.


00:28:26.972 --> 00:28:30.306
It's divided into good parts
and bad parts.


00:28:30.342 --> 00:28:32.909
"Number 1: Wants to do it."


00:28:32.944 --> 00:28:36.746
"Number 2: Has a good chance."


00:28:36.782 --> 00:28:38.849
"Number 3: A lot of people"


00:28:38.884 --> 00:28:41.852
"who know the other candidates
don't like them."


00:28:42.287 --> 00:28:43.420
Um...


00:28:43.455 --> 00:28:44.521
"He might get elected."


00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:46.123
"It would be a good time
to run things."


00:28:46.158 --> 00:28:49.125
"He thinks
he could solve problems."


00:28:49.161 --> 00:28:53.797
"The bad points. Number 1:
He would not be here a lot."


00:28:53.833 --> 00:28:55.865
"Number 2: Uh..."


00:28:55.901 --> 00:28:57.934
"It would be hard to
get more publicity"


00:28:57.969 --> 00:28:59.470
"than some of the others."


00:29:00.272 --> 00:29:01.504
"Number 3:"


00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:05.275
"Would not like to have Social
Security around all the time."


00:29:06.946 --> 00:29:09.146
Meaning the Secret Service,
of course.


00:29:19.458 --> 00:29:22.659
<i>Just moments ago,
I spoke with George W. Bush</i>


00:29:22.694 --> 00:29:24.161
<i>and congratulated him
on becoming</i>


00:29:24.196 --> 00:29:27.297
<i>the 43rd President of
the United States.</i>


00:29:27.333 --> 00:29:31.802
<i>Neither he nor I anticipated
this long and difficult road.</i>


00:29:32.804 --> 00:29:34.537
<i>Let there be no doubt</i>


00:29:34.573 --> 00:29:38.575
<i>while I strongly disagree
with the court's decision,</i>


00:29:38.611 --> 00:29:40.377
<i>I accept it.</i>


00:29:40.412 --> 00:29:42.813
<i>I accept the finality of
this outcome,</i>


00:29:42.848 --> 00:29:44.481
<i>which will be ratified
next Monday</i>


00:29:44.516 --> 00:29:46.449
<i>in the Electoral College.</i>


00:29:46.485 --> 00:29:49.085
<i>And tonight, for the sake of
our unity as a people</i>


00:29:49.121 --> 00:29:51.388
<i>and the strength of
our democracy,</i>


00:29:51.423 --> 00:29:53.490
<i>I offer my concession.</i>


00:29:58.363 --> 00:30:01.565
<i>I had a detailed plan
for my life.</i>


00:30:03.102 --> 00:30:07.304
<i>But it turned out that life
had a different plan for me.</i>


00:30:12.177 --> 00:30:15.244
You know, anybody
who decides to be a part of


00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:17.280
helping to solve
the climate crisis has


00:30:17.315 --> 00:30:21.217
a constant struggle
between hope and despair.


00:30:21.252 --> 00:30:25.888
Uh, and so when you see
these slides about


00:30:25.924 --> 00:30:28.258
all the damage
that's being done, hang on,


00:30:28.293 --> 00:30:31.994
because hope is coming.
Don't sink too low!


00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:35.365
Despair can be paralyzing.


00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.668
But we're building up speed
and we're seeing


00:30:38.704 --> 00:30:43.340
a tremendous amount of
positive change


00:30:43.375 --> 00:30:47.010
that gives me the optimism
that I feel about this.


00:30:47.046 --> 00:30:50.280
Let me give you some exciting
examples. Look at wind.


00:30:50.316 --> 00:30:52.015
One day this month,


00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:55.919
Scotland got 100% of
its electricity from wind.


00:30:55.954 --> 00:30:58.855
In Portugal,
they had four days straight


00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.824
last May on
renewable energy alone.


00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:05.128
And on a global basis,
wind alone can supply


00:31:05.163 --> 00:31:10.433
40 times the entire
global demand for energy.


00:31:10.468 --> 00:31:12.169
But let's look at solar.


00:31:12.204 --> 00:31:15.205
This, to me, is the most
exciting new development.


00:31:15.241 --> 00:31:19.876
Fourteen years ago
the best projections available


00:31:19.911 --> 00:31:23.446
were that we would be able
to install worldwide


00:31:23.482 --> 00:31:27.117
one gigawatt of
solar electricity per year.


00:31:27.152 --> 00:31:29.286
Well, when 2010 arrived,


00:31:29.321 --> 00:31:32.989
we beat that goal by
17 times over.


00:31:33.024 --> 00:31:37.226
Last year, we beat that goal
58 times over.


00:31:37.262 --> 00:31:42.132
This year, we're on track to
beat that goal 73 times over.


00:31:42.167 --> 00:31:46.970
This is an exponential curve,
and it is astonishing!


00:31:47.005 --> 00:31:49.840
And it continues to go up
at a steeper rate


00:31:49.875 --> 00:31:52.843
because the cost of
silicon solar cells


00:31:52.878 --> 00:31:55.312
continues to go down.


00:31:55.347 --> 00:31:59.349
I wish that every state
would encourage solar.


00:31:59.384 --> 00:32:00.416
Including Florida.


00:32:00.452 --> 00:32:03.620
You know, Florida's called
the Sunshine State?


00:32:03.655 --> 00:32:07.290
The head of the second-biggest
fossil fuel utility there


00:32:07.325 --> 00:32:09.660
was actually quoted as saying,


00:32:09.695 --> 00:32:11.061
"Yes, we're the Sunshine State,"


00:32:11.096 --> 00:32:13.496
"but, remember, we're also
the partly cloudy state."


00:32:13.532 --> 00:32:17.234
And they have lobbied
the state legislature


00:32:17.269 --> 00:32:20.503
to basically make it illegal


00:32:20.538 --> 00:32:24.074
to lease a solar panel for
your rooftop from anybody


00:32:24.109 --> 00:32:27.244
but the
fossil fuel-burning utility!


00:32:27.279 --> 00:32:30.246
And they're trying to use
their legacy political power


00:32:30.282 --> 00:32:33.450
to bend the politicians
to their will.


00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:37.620
What the public wants
doesn't seem to matter.


00:32:37.655 --> 00:32:39.089
It's pathetic!


00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:46.863
<i>In
Campaign 2016 segment tonight,</i>


00:32:46.898 --> 00:32:48.431
<i>an interview with Donald Trump.</i>


00:32:48.466 --> 00:32:50.200
<i>Not a believer
in global warming.</i>


00:32:50.235 --> 00:32:52.068
<i>Obama thinks it's
the number one problem</i>


00:32:52.103 --> 00:32:53.536
<i>of the world today,</i>


00:32:53.571 --> 00:32:56.339
<i>and I think
it's very low on the list.</i>


00:32:56.374 --> 00:32:57.974
<i>If you were President,</i>


00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.643
<i>would you invest
a lot of money in</i>


00:33:00.679 --> 00:33:03.346
<i>alternative energy
to get away from fossil fuels?</i>


00:33:03.381 --> 00:33:05.716
<i>Now solar always
sounds better than it is.</i>


00:33:05.751 --> 00:33:08.585
<i>You know, you get
your money back in 36 years.</i>


00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:09.986
<i>The problem is the panels</i>


00:33:10.022 --> 00:33:12.222
<i>destroy themselves
after ten years.</i>


00:33:13.459 --> 00:33:16.226
<i>...you know, one of the top,
top professors at MIT.</i>


00:33:16.261 --> 00:33:17.593
Hey!


00:33:17.629 --> 00:33:20.096
Hey, Mr. Vice President,
thank you so much for coming.


00:33:20.132 --> 00:33:21.564
Thank you.


00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:23.567
<i>So, we're
in relatively early stages</i>


00:33:23.602 --> 00:33:25.101
<i>of the investigation, but</i>


00:33:25.136 --> 00:33:28.037
some of these companies
and the denial organizations


00:33:28.073 --> 00:33:30.073
and groups like the
American Petroleum Institute


00:33:30.109 --> 00:33:32.542
have been actively seeking
to distort the market,


00:33:32.577 --> 00:33:34.944
to suppress investment
in renewables


00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:37.947
through this relatively
new line of propaganda,


00:33:37.982 --> 00:33:39.549
which is,


00:33:39.585 --> 00:33:42.585
"Even if everything"


00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.255
"Al Gore's been saying
since the '80s is true,"


00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:46.333
"it's gonna cost so much money,"


00:33:46.357 --> 00:33:47.757
"it's gonna
cripple the economy."


00:33:47.792 --> 00:33:50.326
It's an insidious theme
that we've seen before,


00:33:50.361 --> 00:33:51.961
but it's coming back
with a new force.


00:33:51.997 --> 00:33:54.631
And I think that


00:33:54.666 --> 00:33:56.544
this is something we're
focusing on very intently.


00:33:56.568 --> 00:33:58.446
We want to know who the groups
are who are doing this,


00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:00.303
what is their analysis,
what's the science,


00:34:00.338 --> 00:34:03.273
and what are the government
actions that are being taken?


00:34:03.308 --> 00:34:04.607
Assuming for the moment


00:34:04.643 --> 00:34:08.011
that the facts prove
that's what they did,


00:34:08.046 --> 00:34:10.080
that's a violation of the law,
isn't it?


00:34:10.115 --> 00:34:11.681
That's consumer
and securities fraud.


00:34:11.717 --> 00:34:15.385
But it's clear that
what they've done is


00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:20.356
essentially tried to cripple


00:34:20.392 --> 00:34:22.959
our ability, mankind's ability,


00:34:22.995 --> 00:34:25.662
to respond
to this existential threat.


00:34:34.238 --> 00:34:35.505
<i>A few days ago,</i>


00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:38.141
<i>the Attorney General of
the State of New York</i>


00:34:38.176 --> 00:34:42.179
<i>launched an investigation</i>
of ExxonMobil


00:34:42.214 --> 00:34:48.284
for fraud and funneling money
to this new form of denial


00:34:48.319 --> 00:34:50.953
where people are out there
minimizing solar, saying,


00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:52.289
"It's not going anywhere."


00:34:52.324 --> 00:34:55.258
"Why are you wasting
your time on this?" Et cetera.


00:34:55.293 --> 00:34:59.195
Utility monopolies really,
really fear disruption,


00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:01.230
and have woken up and said,


00:35:01.265 --> 00:35:03.099
"Wow, this solar thing,"


00:35:03.134 --> 00:35:06.569
"I need to crush it
before it kills me."


00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:08.838
The last two weeks,
we had 55,000 people


00:35:08.874 --> 00:35:11.041
sign up in Nevada to say,
you know,


00:35:11.076 --> 00:35:13.342
"I want choice,
I want energy freedom."


00:35:13.377 --> 00:35:14.878
What happened in Nevada


00:35:14.913 --> 00:35:17.080
will send a massive message
to the rest of the country.


00:35:17.115 --> 00:35:18.882
Yeah, I think so. Yeah.


00:35:23.488 --> 00:35:24.966
<i>Thank you. Will there
be bumps in the road?</i>


00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:27.023
You bet there will be.


00:35:27.058 --> 00:35:29.292
But, at the end of the day,


00:35:30.161 --> 00:35:33.362
you really are
on the front lines.


00:35:33.397 --> 00:35:36.599
On the rooftops, if you will.


00:35:36.634 --> 00:35:41.271
And we're seeing this play out
in every part of the world.


00:35:45.643 --> 00:35:49.613
<i>We've had 150 years of
burning fossil fuels</i>


00:35:49.648 --> 00:35:51.514
<i>with the many blessings,
poverty's gone down,</i>


00:35:51.550 --> 00:35:54.417
living standards have increased.


00:35:54.452 --> 00:35:57.187
So naturally, there still are
some people that say,


00:35:57.222 --> 00:36:00.990
"Well, we just have to keep
relying on oil and gas"


00:36:01.026 --> 00:36:02.559
"and even coal."


00:36:02.594 --> 00:36:04.961
But it is worth pointing out
that in northern China,


00:36:04.996 --> 00:36:08.898
life expectancy has gone down
five and a half years


00:36:08.933 --> 00:36:10.533
because of air pollution.


00:36:10.569 --> 00:36:13.036
And in Beijing, the mayor said,


00:36:13.071 --> 00:36:14.604
"My city is not livable."


00:36:16.007 --> 00:36:18.641
But in many parts of China


00:36:18.676 --> 00:36:21.711
it is already cheaper
to get electricity


00:36:21.747 --> 00:36:23.746
from solar and wind.


00:36:23.782 --> 00:36:27.583
So this is the time
when your presentations


00:36:27.618 --> 00:36:31.221
can provide support for your
government's determination


00:36:31.256 --> 00:36:34.591
to do even better
with renewable energy.


00:36:39.631 --> 00:36:41.231
Fine. How are you?


00:36:41.266 --> 00:36:43.433
<i>There are so many commitments</i>


00:36:43.468 --> 00:36:47.469
that governments are thinking
about making right now


00:36:47.505 --> 00:36:51.440
for hundreds of new coal plants.


00:36:51.476 --> 00:36:56.812
Even as the revolutionary drop
in cost from renewables


00:36:56.848 --> 00:36:58.380
is picking up speed.


00:36:58.416 --> 00:37:00.216
China's one of these countries.


00:37:00.251 --> 00:37:03.552
And there are numbers of
others. India, others.


00:37:03.588 --> 00:37:07.090
Unfortunately, they've looked
at the price of coal.


00:37:07.125 --> 00:37:08.625
They see the price low,


00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:10.627
and they feel they're compelled


00:37:10.662 --> 00:37:13.563
therefore to try to provide
for their people.


00:37:13.598 --> 00:37:17.200
So if India proceeds
to build the number of plants


00:37:17.235 --> 00:37:20.169
it is currently planning,
and they are coal fired,


00:37:20.205 --> 00:37:22.639
it can erase everything
everybody else


00:37:22.674 --> 00:37:24.674
is trying to do
in terms of reductions.


00:37:24.709 --> 00:37:26.876
We've got to figure out
a way to crack that nut.


00:37:26.911 --> 00:37:27.911
Absolutely.


00:37:32.851 --> 00:37:34.149
<i>Now, climate change is high</i>


00:37:34.185 --> 00:37:35.518
<i>on world leaders' agendas.</i>


00:37:35.553 --> 00:37:38.388
<i>And that's because the crucial
U.N. talks in Paris</i>


00:37:38.423 --> 00:37:40.489
<i>start in less than two months,</i>


00:37:40.525 --> 00:37:42.725
<i>when the world gets together
to try to agree</i>


00:37:42.761 --> 00:37:43.859
<i>on a global deal.</i>


00:37:43.895 --> 00:37:46.596
<i>One of the sticking points
remains financing.</i>


00:37:48.599 --> 00:37:51.500
<i>My agenda is dominated right now</i>


00:37:51.536 --> 00:37:54.871
<i>by the preparations for the
climate conference in Paris.</i>


00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:58.975
<i>The world's been preparing
for it a long time.</i>


00:38:03.614 --> 00:38:06.449
<i>And the main objective is
to speed up</i>


00:38:06.485 --> 00:38:08.517
<i>the transition
to renewable energy,</i>


00:38:08.553 --> 00:38:11.320
<i>so that the entire world</i>


00:38:11.356 --> 00:38:14.691
<i>can bring down
the pollution levels</i>


00:38:14.726 --> 00:38:18.061
while continuing
to reduce poverty.


00:38:18.096 --> 00:38:21.330
<i>But there is still a big divide</i>


00:38:21.365 --> 00:38:23.867
<i>between rich countries
and poor countries.</i>


00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:27.203
So, thank you
for arranging this,


00:38:27.239 --> 00:38:30.539
and for the privilege of
talking with you.


00:38:30.575 --> 00:38:33.876
As you know,
I have this passion for


00:38:33.912 --> 00:38:38.515
solving the climate crisis
that goes back 40 years. And...


00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:43.786
Because I have studied it
so closely, I'm hoping that


00:38:43.822 --> 00:38:47.389
the United States and India
will accelerate


00:38:47.425 --> 00:38:52.494
our transitions to
renewable energy together.


00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:54.464
India has always looked
at the United States


00:38:54.499 --> 00:38:57.967
as a valuable partner.


00:38:58.002 --> 00:39:02.905
But sadly, the Western world,
the developed world


00:39:02.941 --> 00:39:06.442
does not seem
to be coming forth with


00:39:06.478 --> 00:39:08.878
significant amounts of support.


00:39:08.914 --> 00:39:11.847
And seems to be creating
more impediments.


00:39:11.883 --> 00:39:15.051
All this talk about supporting
climate change


00:39:15.086 --> 00:39:16.252
seems to be only talk,


00:39:16.287 --> 00:39:18.421
and there's hardly
any action on that.


00:39:18.456 --> 00:39:20.356
- In the U.S.?
- Yeah, from the U.S.


00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:21.724
I don't think it's fair,


00:39:21.759 --> 00:39:23.359
with all due respect,
if you may?


00:39:23.394 --> 00:39:26.296
- Sure, please.
- Well, in the U.S.


00:39:26.331 --> 00:39:30.266
in calendar year, uh, 2015,


00:39:30.301 --> 00:39:32.835
if you look at the investments
in the U.S.


00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:36.939
in the building of new, uh,
electric generating capacity,


00:39:36.975 --> 00:39:39.675
three-quarters is
from solar and wind.


00:39:39.711 --> 00:39:41.745
- May I respond to that?
- Yes. Please.


00:39:42.981 --> 00:39:46.215
I'll do the same thing
after 150 years.


00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:48.117
After I've used my coal.


00:39:48.153 --> 00:39:49.919
After I've got my people jobs.


00:39:49.955 --> 00:39:51.954
After I've created
my infrastructure


00:39:51.989 --> 00:39:53.923
and highways and roads.


00:39:53.958 --> 00:39:55.524
When I have technology.


00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:56.860
When my people earn


00:39:56.895 --> 00:39:59.829
$50,000-$70,000
per capita income,


00:39:59.864 --> 00:40:03.332
using low-cost
fossil fuels-based energy.


00:40:03.368 --> 00:40:06.136
The way the United States did
for 150 years.


00:40:07.438 --> 00:40:09.305
It's very easy to say now that,


00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:11.073
"Oh, we are not using coal!"


00:40:11.108 --> 00:40:12.708
What about in the past?


00:40:12.744 --> 00:40:15.111
So I'm only asking for
that carbon space


00:40:15.147 --> 00:40:18.514
which you utilized
for 150 years.


00:40:18.549 --> 00:40:22.017
My point is not
to deny your right


00:40:22.053 --> 00:40:25.454
to make your own choices as to
what kind of energy you want.


00:40:25.489 --> 00:40:27.390
Obviously you have that right.


00:40:27.425 --> 00:40:29.725
But what I am saying,


00:40:29.761 --> 00:40:32.061
when is the sun coming up today?


00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:35.130
I don't see it anywhere.


00:40:35.166 --> 00:40:36.900
I don't see the blue sky.


00:40:48.479 --> 00:40:51.046
<i>When any of us walk outside</i>


00:40:51.082 --> 00:40:52.782
<i>and look up at the sky,</i>


00:40:52.817 --> 00:40:55.318
<i>our natural impression is</i>


00:40:55.353 --> 00:41:01.057
<i>that the sky looks like it's
a vast and limitless expanse.</i>


00:41:01.092 --> 00:41:03.593
<i>Goes on forever.</i>


00:41:03.628 --> 00:41:06.061
<i>Actually, the atmosphere
of the Earth</i>


00:41:06.097 --> 00:41:11.034
<i>is a very thin shell
surrounding the planet.</i>


00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:14.170
<i>And, of course, right now</i>


00:41:14.205 --> 00:41:18.941
we are putting 110 million tons


00:41:18.976 --> 00:41:21.844
of heat-trapping
global-warming pollution


00:41:21.879 --> 00:41:26.248
into that space
every single day.


00:41:26.284 --> 00:41:29.752
We're using this
as an open sewer


00:41:29.787 --> 00:41:34.523
for all of the gaseous waste
in our global civilization.


00:41:34.559 --> 00:41:37.860
Agriculture is
a big cause of it.


00:41:37.895 --> 00:41:41.097
Burning of forests and
burning of cropland.


00:41:41.132 --> 00:41:44.900
But still, the main part of
the problem


00:41:44.936 --> 00:41:47.570
is the emission of
carbon dioxide


00:41:47.605 --> 00:41:50.806
from the burning of
coal, oil, and gas.


00:41:50.842 --> 00:41:54.944
And that builds up heat energy
and raises temperatures.


00:41:56.614 --> 00:41:59.815
<i>India just set their
all-time high temperature</i>


00:41:59.851 --> 00:42:04.787
<i>record in May.
123.8 degrees Fahrenheit.</i>


00:42:06.357 --> 00:42:08.591
<i>The streets are melting.</i>


00:42:09.527 --> 00:42:12.728
<i>We have built
a civilization for conditions</i>


00:42:12.764 --> 00:42:16.633
<i>that we are now in the process
of radically changing.</i>


00:42:17.702 --> 00:42:19.802
All-time records have
been broken this year


00:42:19.837 --> 00:42:23.272
in Thailand and Cambodia
and Laos.


00:42:23.307 --> 00:42:27.910
In Pakistan, over 1,200 people
died in the heat wave there.


00:42:27.946 --> 00:42:32.948
This year, they have dug
anticipatory mass graves


00:42:32.984 --> 00:42:35.217
for the people
they fear will die


00:42:35.252 --> 00:42:37.953
in this year's heat waves.


00:42:37.989 --> 00:42:42.425
And we're seeing that
the higher temperatures


00:42:42.460 --> 00:42:46.329
are shifting the balance
between microbes


00:42:46.364 --> 00:42:48.197
and human beings.


00:42:48.232 --> 00:42:51.334
The transportation revolution
has a lot to do with this.


00:42:51.369 --> 00:42:52.668
Air travel.


00:42:52.703 --> 00:42:56.806
But the climate conditions
have a big impact on that.


00:42:56.841 --> 00:42:58.774
Let's look at Zika.


00:42:58.809 --> 00:43:01.978
Here is the range of
the one mosquito


00:43:02.013 --> 00:43:03.813
that they're most worried about.


00:43:03.848 --> 00:43:06.382
And warmer conditions


00:43:06.417 --> 00:43:09.985
increase that range
considerably.


00:43:10.021 --> 00:43:12.821
But here is the kicker on this.


00:43:12.857 --> 00:43:15.624
It's not just the mosquito,
it's the virus.


00:43:15.659 --> 00:43:18.795
And the warmer temperatures
speed up


00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:22.031
the incubation rate
inside the mosquito.


00:43:22.066 --> 00:43:26.736
So we get an explosion
in the number of cases.


00:43:26.771 --> 00:43:29.639
And now it's spread
to Miami, Florida.


00:43:29.674 --> 00:43:33.008
And for the first time
in history,


00:43:33.044 --> 00:43:35.111
pregnant women have been advised


00:43:35.146 --> 00:43:38.348
not to go to part of
the United States of America.


00:43:39.751 --> 00:43:40.817
In many areas of


00:43:40.852 --> 00:43:42.952
Central America
and South America,


00:43:42.987 --> 00:43:44.820
the doctors are delivering
a message


00:43:44.855 --> 00:43:46.288
that I've never heard
in my life.


00:43:46.324 --> 00:43:49.324
They're telling women,
"Don't get pregnant"


00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:52.729
"for two years, while we try
to get a handle on this."


00:43:52.764 --> 00:43:56.432
That's something new in
the history of the human race.


00:43:56.468 --> 00:43:58.401
How long can we just
sit back and say,


00:43:58.436 --> 00:44:02.405
"Oh, well, maybe some genius
will think up some miracle"?


00:44:02.440 --> 00:44:04.841
I'm sorry, I'm getting all, um,


00:44:06.578 --> 00:44:08.343
fired up here.


00:44:14.886 --> 00:44:18.387
But let's step back
and take a global view


00:44:18.422 --> 00:44:22.057
of the increasing temperatures
and the extra heat energy.


00:44:22.093 --> 00:44:27.796
93% of all this heat energy
is going into the oceans.


00:44:27.832 --> 00:44:29.398
And it has several consequences.


00:44:29.433 --> 00:44:31.066
A direct consequence is


00:44:31.101 --> 00:44:36.172
that when ocean-based storms
cross much warmer ocean waters


00:44:36.207 --> 00:44:39.675
the storms get stronger
and more destructive.


00:44:39.711 --> 00:44:40.842
Just a few years ago


00:44:40.878 --> 00:44:43.379
Superstorm Sandy in the Atlantic


00:44:43.414 --> 00:44:47.516
crossed areas of the ocean
9 degrees Fahrenheit


00:44:47.552 --> 00:44:49.752
warmer than normal.


00:44:49.787 --> 00:44:52.454
And it caused
tremendous destruction


00:44:52.489 --> 00:44:55.291
in New Jersey and in New York.


00:44:55.326 --> 00:44:57.760
And, by the way, ten years ago,


00:44:57.795 --> 00:44:59.896
when the movie
<i>An Inconvenient Truth</i>


00:44:59.931 --> 00:45:04.734
came out, the single most
criticized scene in that movie


00:45:04.769 --> 00:45:07.770
<i>was an animated scene showing</i>


00:45:07.805 --> 00:45:11.240
<i>that the combination of
sea level rise and storm surge</i>


00:45:11.275 --> 00:45:16.812
<i>would put the ocean water
into the 9/11 memorial site,</i>


00:45:16.848 --> 00:45:19.148
<i>which was then
under construction.</i>


00:45:19.183 --> 00:45:20.349
<i>And people said,</i>


00:45:20.385 --> 00:45:24.087
<i>"That's ridiculous.
What a terrible exaggeration!"</i>


00:45:25.322 --> 00:45:27.189
<i>Something happened last night</i>


00:45:27.224 --> 00:45:29.926
<i>at one of the most iconic
locations in New York</i>


00:45:29.961 --> 00:45:32.661
<i>the World Trade Center,
Ground Zero.</i>


00:45:32.697 --> 00:45:35.865
<i>A flood of water
with a current so strong</i>


00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:38.266
<i>it flooded the reconstruction.</i>


00:45:38.302 --> 00:45:40.135
<i>There is a wakeup call here,</i>


00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:46.108
<i>and that is climate change
and our vulnerability to it.</i>


00:45:46.144 --> 00:45:49.578
It was true ten years ago,
it was true five years ago.


00:45:49.614 --> 00:45:52.014
<i>It is undeniable today.</i>


00:45:57.422 --> 00:45:58.487
Years before,


00:45:58.523 --> 00:46:02.024
the scientists predicted that
this would happen.


00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:03.926
One year later,


00:46:03.961 --> 00:46:06.495
in the Philippines,
Super Typhoon Haiyan


00:46:06.530 --> 00:46:08.330
crossed areas of the Pacific


00:46:08.365 --> 00:46:11.467
three degrees Celsius
warmer than normal.


00:46:11.503 --> 00:46:12.802
And it became


00:46:12.837 --> 00:46:15.838
the strongest and most
destructive ocean-based storm


00:46:15.873 --> 00:46:17.773
ever to make landfall.


00:46:17.809 --> 00:46:20.910
Uh, in the city of Tacloban
and nearby areas


00:46:20.945 --> 00:46:24.981
there were 4.1 million
climate refugees.


00:46:26.217 --> 00:46:28.818
Thousands of people were killed.


00:46:44.302 --> 00:46:45.834
<i>Even just remembering it now,</i>


00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:49.104
after more than two years,


00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:52.174
just remembering
the sound of the wind.


00:47:01.051 --> 00:47:03.018
<i>And then the water came after.</i>


00:47:03.053 --> 00:47:06.222
Like a wall of water
just rushing in.


00:47:16.934 --> 00:47:19.635
That's why we had to
climb to the roof.


00:47:19.671 --> 00:47:22.171
In fact, I was shouting,
giving them orders


00:47:22.206 --> 00:47:23.416
"Break that ceiling, we've gotta"


00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:25.120
"get out of here
and we've gotta climb up."


00:47:39.924 --> 00:47:42.391
<i>If we went to any other place,
we would have died.</i>


00:47:42.427 --> 00:47:45.094
<i>Because that had
a concrete structure,</i>


00:47:45.129 --> 00:47:47.630
<i>and that's where
we were able to climb up.</i>


00:47:56.473 --> 00:47:59.608
<i>People are running, are crying</i>


00:47:59.643 --> 00:48:03.145
<i>because they don't know
what happened to their family.</i>


00:48:06.149 --> 00:48:08.985
<i>We see a lot of dead people.</i>


00:48:09.787 --> 00:48:12.788
I saw Sir Alfred helping...


00:48:12.824 --> 00:48:16.492
Helping, uh,
the other people to, to...


00:48:16.527 --> 00:48:18.094
What do you call this? To...


00:48:18.129 --> 00:48:20.162
- Recover the bodies?
- To recover the bodies.


00:48:21.298 --> 00:48:22.531
Then...


00:48:22.567 --> 00:48:23.499
Sorry.


00:48:23.534 --> 00:48:25.467
No, that's okay. It's okay.


00:48:25.502 --> 00:48:27.603
Thank God you're safe.


00:48:27.638 --> 00:48:30.940
Actually, sir, I am so scared


00:48:30.975 --> 00:48:32.875
when I'm telling this
to my friend...


00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:34.021
It brings it back, doesn't it?


00:48:34.045 --> 00:48:37.580
Yes, sir. I felt so, so scared.


00:49:36.907 --> 00:49:39.341
<i>When Tacloban was hit,</i>


00:49:39.376 --> 00:49:43.712
<i>I knew that I wanted
to organize a training here</i>


00:49:43.748 --> 00:49:45.214
<i>in the Philippines</i>


00:49:47.184 --> 00:49:49.285
<i>to meet face-to-face
with the people</i>


00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:53.221
<i>who were on the front lines
of this and empower them</i>


00:49:53.257 --> 00:49:58.160
<i>to translate an excruciatingly
painful experience</i>


00:49:58.195 --> 00:50:02.565
<i>into a focus on changing policy,</i>


00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:04.633
<i>confronting public officials.</i>


00:50:06.571 --> 00:50:10.005
I don't know
any other way to do it.


00:50:10.041 --> 00:50:14.610
The loss of life
was very tragic.


00:50:14.645 --> 00:50:19.849
What a blessing it was that
Pope Francis came to Tacloban


00:50:19.884 --> 00:50:22.417
to the ground zero
of that tragedy,


00:50:22.452 --> 00:50:24.386
and delivered
a very powerful message that


00:50:24.421 --> 00:50:26.888
"the gravest effects of
the climate crisis"


00:50:26.924 --> 00:50:31.126
"are visited upon
the poorest people."


00:50:31.162 --> 00:50:34.163
This is true
everywhere in the world.


00:50:37.101 --> 00:50:39.001
Now, the second order
consequences


00:50:39.036 --> 00:50:40.502
of the warming oceans


00:50:40.538 --> 00:50:42.905
include some that
we're all experiencing


00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:45.040
on a regular basis now.


00:50:45.075 --> 00:50:47.342
When the temperature goes up,


00:50:47.377 --> 00:50:50.246
the water vapor coming off
the oceans


00:50:50.281 --> 00:50:54.416
into the sky
is increasing significantly.


00:50:54.452 --> 00:50:59.521
This means that
every storm is different now


00:50:59.556 --> 00:51:04.627
because it takes place
in a warmer and wetter world.


00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:09.764
Water vapor is often funneled


00:51:09.800 --> 00:51:13.702
thousands of kilometers
from the oceans over the land,


00:51:13.738 --> 00:51:18.374
and then much more of it
falls at the same time.


00:51:19.543 --> 00:51:23.412
Look at this downpour
that hit Tucson, Arizona,


00:51:23.447 --> 00:51:26.749
and watch the water splash
off the city.


00:51:28.319 --> 00:51:32.121
They're now calling
these things "rain bombs."


00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:36.058
Houston's been hit
between May of last year


00:51:36.093 --> 00:51:38.227
and May of this year.


00:51:38.262 --> 00:51:43.565
Two 1-in-500-year floods and
one 1-in-1,000-year downpour.


00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:47.536
Now, that's unusual.
Almost Noah-like.


00:51:49.206 --> 00:51:51.507
This was in Spain last fall.


00:51:52.877 --> 00:51:56.445
In Chile, last year. Epic event.


00:51:57.781 --> 00:52:00.415
And now, today,


00:52:00.451 --> 00:52:03.185
there are water rescues underway


00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:06.655
in southeast Louisiana.


00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:08.691
<i>Give me a knife,
give me a knife.</i>


00:52:27.678 --> 00:52:30.512
Now, the same heat


00:52:30.547 --> 00:52:33.515
that is bringing all that
water vapor off the oceans


00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:36.752
is sucking the soil moisture
out of the ground.


00:52:36.787 --> 00:52:40.756
And it's making the droughts
deeper and longer.


00:52:41.625 --> 00:52:44.759
In China, in Vietnam,
in Thailand,


00:52:44.795 --> 00:52:47.196
and all around the world.


00:52:47.231 --> 00:52:49.965
And where there is drought,


00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:52.067
the vegetation dries out


00:52:52.102 --> 00:52:54.570
and the fires increase.


00:52:57.474 --> 00:52:59.975
<i>Every night on the evening
news is like a nature hike</i>


00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:02.444
<i>through the Book of Revelations.</i>


00:53:02.479 --> 00:53:07.183
<i>But the dots are very seldom
connected in the media.</i>


00:53:07.952 --> 00:53:10.785
<i>This is global warming.</i>


00:53:10.821 --> 00:53:15.357
<i>When we have scientists
tell us the dots connect,</i>


00:53:15.392 --> 00:53:19.995
we need to have in
our democracy a conversation


00:53:20.030 --> 00:53:21.930
about cause and effect.


00:53:21.965 --> 00:53:23.264
Let's look, for example,


00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:25.868
at the story of
what has happened in Syria.


00:53:27.137 --> 00:53:30.372
From 2006 to 2010,


00:53:30.407 --> 00:53:32.740
they had
a record-breaking drought.


00:53:32.776 --> 00:53:37.146
This farmer is one of many
who lost his farm.


00:53:37.181 --> 00:53:41.449
60% of all the farms in Syria
were destroyed.


00:53:41.485 --> 00:53:44.987
80% of all their livestock
were killed.


00:53:46.257 --> 00:53:47.956
<i>A climate-exacerbated drought</i>


00:53:47.991 --> 00:53:49.725
<i>during that period contributed</i>


00:53:49.760 --> 00:53:52.094
<i>to the displacement
of about 2 million people</i>


00:53:52.129 --> 00:53:54.529
<i>before the conflict broke out.</i>


00:53:54.565 --> 00:53:56.531
<i>Yes, many other causes.</i>


00:53:56.566 --> 00:53:58.567
<i>The dictator there is a bad guy,</i>


00:53:58.603 --> 00:54:01.603
<i>it's a multi-sided civil war,
and all of the rest.</i>


00:54:01.638 --> 00:54:06.374
But this drought is the worst
in at least 900 years.


00:54:06.409 --> 00:54:09.044
As far back as the records go.


00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.046
It is unprecedented!


00:54:11.081 --> 00:54:12.447
<i>And, of course,</i>


00:54:12.483 --> 00:54:14.315
<i>since then the country
has been plunged</i>


00:54:14.351 --> 00:54:16.685
<i>into horrific civil war.</i>


00:54:19.556 --> 00:54:21.356
<i>The next generation,</i>


00:54:21.392 --> 00:54:23.692
<i>if they live in a world</i>


00:54:23.727 --> 00:54:26.828
<i>of floods and storms
and rising seas</i>


00:54:26.863 --> 00:54:29.865
<i>and droughts and refugees
by the millions</i>


00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:32.267
<i>escaping unlivable conditions,</i>


00:54:32.302 --> 00:54:36.504
<i>destabilizing countries
around the world,</i>


00:54:36.539 --> 00:54:38.173
<i>they would be well-justified</i>


00:54:38.208 --> 00:54:39.975
<i>in looking back at us
and asking,</i>


00:54:40.010 --> 00:54:41.677
"What were you thinking?"


00:54:43.781 --> 00:54:45.881
"Couldn't you hear what
the scientists were saying?"


00:54:45.916 --> 00:54:49.385
"Couldn't you hear what Mother
Nature was screaming at you?"


00:55:04.501 --> 00:55:06.435
<i>Um, so, first off, Mr. Gore...</i>


00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:09.204
<i>Tell us why you're here.
What's going on today?</i>


00:55:09.239 --> 00:55:12.875
<i>This is a 24-hour
global broadcast</i>


00:55:12.910 --> 00:55:15.910
<i>that is covered live
in every nation</i>


00:55:15.946 --> 00:55:17.479
<i>around the world.</i>


00:55:17.514 --> 00:55:21.884
<i>And we are attempting
to further mobilize</i>


00:55:21.919 --> 00:55:24.219
people in every nation


00:55:24.255 --> 00:55:28.590
to support even more
ambitious outcomes


00:55:28.626 --> 00:55:32.194
in the negotiation that
will take place here in Paris


00:55:32.229 --> 00:55:33.862
just two weeks from now.


00:55:42.806 --> 00:55:45.374
We are on air
in four minutes, please!


00:55:46.043 --> 00:55:47.810
Rio, are you there?


00:55:50.914 --> 00:55:52.947
<i>Mr. Vice President,
welcome to the stage.</i>


00:55:52.983 --> 00:55:54.416
<i>Thank you very much</i>


00:55:54.451 --> 00:55:57.452
<i>and welcome, everyone,
to this global broadcast.</i>


00:55:57.488 --> 00:55:59.254
So, as I stand here today,


00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:01.456
now just two weeks
from the opening of


00:56:01.491 --> 00:56:03.191
the United Nations Conference


00:56:03.227 --> 00:56:05.026
on Climate Change here in Paris,


00:56:05.061 --> 00:56:06.494
what we need now is for people


00:56:06.530 --> 00:56:08.831
all over the world to speak out.


00:56:08.866 --> 00:56:13.368
<i>And that's what the next
24 hours are all about.</i>


00:56:42.565 --> 00:56:44.967
Stand by.
We're gonna come to the toss.


00:56:46.269 --> 00:56:47.569
We're on you. Go.


00:56:47.605 --> 00:56:49.337
We've got to say to Mr. Gore


00:56:49.373 --> 00:56:50.933
we'll cut this short
to get to Pharrell.


00:56:51.876 --> 00:56:54.576
I'll get it settled.
Just don't do anything rash.


00:57:00.784 --> 00:57:03.419
Can we cut it down?
Can we take it out early?


00:57:10.561 --> 00:57:12.427
<i>They've asked us
to bring your car over</i>


00:57:12.462 --> 00:57:15.097
by the trailer just in case


00:57:15.132 --> 00:57:16.398
we need to get you out of here.


00:57:16.433 --> 00:57:17.966
Okay. Is it terrorist related?


00:57:18.001 --> 00:57:19.001
They don't know yet.


00:57:19.035 --> 00:57:20.547
Probably, because
there's several shootings


00:57:20.571 --> 00:57:22.037
around Paris.


00:57:22.072 --> 00:57:23.972
There's several dead people.


00:57:24.007 --> 00:57:26.575
And, uh, so it's pretty sure
it's terrorism.


00:57:28.879 --> 00:57:30.445
"Police officials in France say"


00:57:30.481 --> 00:57:31.691
"there's been
an explosion in a bar"


00:57:31.715 --> 00:57:33.014
"near a Paris stadium"


00:57:33.050 --> 00:57:35.917
"and a shootout
in a Paris restaurant."


00:57:35.952 --> 00:57:38.786
"President Hollande evacuated
from Stade de France."


00:57:38.822 --> 00:57:40.822
And there are apparently


00:57:40.858 --> 00:57:45.928
18 dead, and AFP is reporting
hostages at the concert hall.


00:57:45.963 --> 00:57:47.462
- Hostages?
- Yes.


00:57:47.497 --> 00:57:49.263
That's all coming from police.


00:57:49.299 --> 00:57:50.566
Wow.


00:58:05.015 --> 00:58:06.548
<i>Paris terror fear.</i>


00:58:06.584 --> 00:58:08.784
<i>Multiple people are reported
killed in a shooting.</i>


00:58:08.819 --> 00:58:11.119
<i>There's also word
of possible explosions...</i>


00:58:11.154 --> 00:58:14.222
<i>Paris is under
an effective police state.</i>


00:58:14.257 --> 00:58:15.957
<i>A curfew is in effect.</i>


00:58:15.992 --> 00:58:17.203
<i>Police don't seem
to have a full handle</i>


00:58:17.227 --> 00:58:18.994
<i>exactly what's going on.</i>


00:58:19.029 --> 00:58:21.496
<i>But this is exactly
the kind of terror scenario</i>


00:58:21.531 --> 00:58:23.732
<i>that the U.S. has long feared.</i>


00:58:27.104 --> 00:58:28.303
<i>Okay.</i>


00:58:28.338 --> 00:58:30.138
Before I go on
to make my statement,


00:58:30.173 --> 00:58:31.606
I just want to say something.


00:58:31.641 --> 00:58:35.677
Those of us who are Americans
stand with you.


00:58:35.713 --> 00:58:40.048
We express
our heartfelt condolences


00:58:40.084 --> 00:58:44.052
for the tragedy here in
the city and in your country.


00:58:44.088 --> 00:58:48.123
This scourge of terrorism
in our world...


00:58:48.158 --> 00:58:49.224
Um...


00:58:54.764 --> 00:58:57.232
We have to defeat this.


00:58:57.267 --> 00:59:01.002
But we have to defeat it
not only with force of arms,


00:59:01.038 --> 00:59:03.738
but with the force
of our values.


00:59:03.773 --> 00:59:05.406
Caring about the future


00:59:05.442 --> 00:59:08.076
and doing what the world
needs to do.


00:59:08.878 --> 00:59:11.980
But, for now, I just wanted to


00:59:12.015 --> 00:59:13.415
say to all of you,


00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:15.783
especially those of you
from France,


00:59:15.819 --> 00:59:17.652
what's in my heart
is in the hearts


00:59:17.687 --> 00:59:19.854
of all the Americans here who


00:59:19.890 --> 00:59:23.659
love you and care about you
and stand with you.


00:59:39.142 --> 00:59:40.541
Ladies and gentlemen,


00:59:40.577 --> 00:59:43.945
we are suspending our broadcast


00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:48.216
<i>because of the tragedies
that have unfolded here.</i>


01:00:13.343 --> 01:00:14.876
<i>The fact that those</i>


01:00:14.912 --> 01:00:17.813
<i>terrible attacks
took place in the city</i>


01:00:17.848 --> 01:00:21.216
<i>where the climate conference
was about to begin</i>


01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:28.456
<i>caused many to connect
these two events</i>


01:00:28.492 --> 01:00:31.159
<i>at a deep level
that is difficult</i>


01:00:31.195 --> 01:00:34.229
<i>to articulate in words.</i>


01:00:36.766 --> 01:00:38.667
<i>There have been</i>


01:00:38.702 --> 01:00:43.405
<i>so many times when
big setbacks have tempted me</i>


01:00:43.440 --> 01:00:45.140
<i>to deep despair.</i>


01:00:47.844 --> 01:00:51.980
<i>But if I can draw
upon my faith tradition,</i>


01:00:53.483 --> 01:00:56.451
<i>God said, "I lay before you"</i>


01:00:56.487 --> 01:01:00.822
<i>"a choice
between life and death."</i>


01:01:02.259 --> 01:01:03.992
<i>"Therefore, choose life."</i>


01:01:33.723 --> 01:01:35.257
<i>We have come to Paris</i>


01:01:35.926 --> 01:01:37.692
<i>to show our resolve.</i>


01:01:37.728 --> 01:01:40.995
<i>We offer our condolences,
and we salute</i>


01:01:41.031 --> 01:01:43.564
<i>the people of Paris
for insisting</i>


01:01:43.599 --> 01:01:45.434
this crucial conference go on.


01:01:46.804 --> 01:01:49.103
<i>To show the world we
are a global family</i>


01:01:49.138 --> 01:01:51.072
<i>in solidarity
with the French people.</i>


01:01:51.107 --> 01:01:52.440
<i>On behalf
of the Romanian people,</i>


01:01:52.476 --> 01:01:53.708
<i>our deepest sympathies.</i>


01:01:53.743 --> 01:01:54.976
<i>We show our sympathy</i>


01:01:55.012 --> 01:01:56.511
<i>and solidarity
with the government</i>


01:01:56.546 --> 01:01:57.913
<i>and people of France.</i>


01:02:02.786 --> 01:02:04.719
Is there a subway stop near here


01:02:04.754 --> 01:02:07.022
where we could take the subway?


01:02:13.363 --> 01:02:14.363
All right, so...


01:02:14.397 --> 01:02:16.197
Okay. And I'll meet you
at Le Bourget?


01:02:16.233 --> 01:02:18.867
Yeah, we'll meet you
at Le Bourget. Let's go.


01:02:28.511 --> 01:02:29.511
<i>Great. Welcome.</i>


01:02:32.749 --> 01:02:33.949
Yeah, yeah.


01:02:36.453 --> 01:02:37.453
No. Not anymore.


01:02:45.228 --> 01:02:47.828
I wish you were
elected president.


01:02:47.864 --> 01:02:49.344
I wish I could call you
Mr. President.


01:02:53.403 --> 01:02:55.302
- Thank you. All right.
- Thank you, thank you.


01:02:55.338 --> 01:02:56.471
Thank you.


01:02:56.506 --> 01:02:57.826
Okay. All right,
nice to meet you.


01:02:59.476 --> 01:03:00.776
Oh, thank you!


01:03:04.914 --> 01:03:06.481
Mr. Vice President.


01:03:06.516 --> 01:03:08.616
- Good to see you again.
- Congratulations in person.


01:03:08.651 --> 01:03:10.085
Thank you very much, sir.


01:03:10.120 --> 01:03:13.788
And thanks for the change
you're bringing about.


01:03:13.824 --> 01:03:14.967
- Good luck to you.
- I appreciate that.


01:03:14.991 --> 01:03:16.591
It's Canadians, not me,
but thank you.


01:03:16.627 --> 01:03:17.959
All right.


01:03:17.995 --> 01:03:21.229
<i>To the development
of a low-carbon economy,</i>


01:03:21.265 --> 01:03:26.668
our government is making
climate change a top priority.


01:03:49.826 --> 01:03:54.963
Never before has
a responsibility so great


01:03:54.998 --> 01:03:57.599
been in the hands of so few.


01:04:00.137 --> 01:04:02.804
<i>One of the secrets
of the human condition</i>


01:04:02.839 --> 01:04:05.873
<i>is that suffering
binds us together.</i>


01:04:05.909 --> 01:04:08.643
<i>Those 150 heads of state</i>


01:04:08.678 --> 01:04:11.612
<i>were moved to speak</i>


01:04:11.648 --> 01:04:15.483
<i>in ways I don't think
they otherwise would have.</i>


01:04:16.586 --> 01:04:19.487
<i>Every one of them began</i>


01:04:19.522 --> 01:04:24.392
<i>with, first, condolences,
and then solidarity.</i>


01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:29.830
<i>And when they turned,
in the next paragraph,</i>


01:04:29.866 --> 01:04:31.966
<i>to their hopes
for the conference,</i>


01:04:32.001 --> 01:04:33.968
<i>they could not help but say</i>


01:04:34.003 --> 01:04:36.204
<i>this is an opportunity</i>


01:04:36.239 --> 01:04:41.009
<i>for us to make that
solidarity tangible and real.</i>


01:04:41.878 --> 01:04:43.978
<i>I now give the floor to</i>


01:04:44.014 --> 01:04:46.681
<i>Mr. Narendra Modi,</i>


01:04:46.716 --> 01:04:48.316
Prime Minister of India.


01:04:49.886 --> 01:04:51.419
Democratic India


01:04:52.622 --> 01:04:54.956
must grow rapidly


01:04:55.658 --> 01:04:57.992
to meet the aspirations


01:04:58.027 --> 01:05:04.131
of 1.25 billion people,
300 million of whom


01:05:04.166 --> 01:05:07.102
are without access to energy.


01:05:08.704 --> 01:05:12.607
Energy is a basic human need.


01:05:12.642 --> 01:05:15.876
And there should be no place


01:05:15.912 --> 01:05:18.279
for unilateral steps


01:05:18.314 --> 01:05:22.417
that become
economic barriers for others.


01:05:23.487 --> 01:05:28.089
So, we still need
conventional energy,


01:05:29.359 --> 01:05:30.692
fossil fuel.


01:05:31.394 --> 01:05:32.560
And anything else


01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:36.097
will be morally wrong.


01:05:36.866 --> 01:05:38.166
<i>Thank you.</i>


01:05:41.771 --> 01:05:43.004
Hmm.


01:05:46.742 --> 01:05:49.410
<i>You know, I've been
to all of these different</i>


01:05:49.445 --> 01:05:52.380
<i>climate conferences since 1992.</i>


01:05:53.583 --> 01:05:57.084
We parliamentarians
have an obligation


01:05:57.120 --> 01:05:59.420
to accelerate the movement


01:05:59.456 --> 01:06:02.890
toward meaningful changes
in policies


01:06:02.925 --> 01:06:05.760
in every nation
on the face of this Earth


01:06:05.795 --> 01:06:10.631
to stop the destruction of
the global ecological system.


01:06:12.068 --> 01:06:15.536
<i>It began with a great deal
of optimism in Rio...</i>


01:06:15.572 --> 01:06:16.804
Hello, Mr. Prime Minister.


01:06:16.839 --> 01:06:19.674
<i>...but it soon bogged down.</i>


01:06:19.709 --> 01:06:21.943
<i>The process
used to bring nations together</i>


01:06:21.979 --> 01:06:25.446
to discuss our joint response
to climate change


01:06:25.482 --> 01:06:27.515
is an important one.


01:06:27.551 --> 01:06:30.151
<i>But a growing population
requires more energy</i>


01:06:30.186 --> 01:06:32.019
<i>to heat and cool our homes.</i>


01:06:32.054 --> 01:06:34.122
<i>More gas to drive our cars.</i>


01:06:35.458 --> 01:06:38.592
<i>For all that time,
the world has struggled</i>


01:06:38.628 --> 01:06:41.629
<i>- to get its act together...</i>
- Oh! Hey!


01:06:41.665 --> 01:06:44.866
<i>...and connect the science
to the policy.</i>


01:06:44.901 --> 01:06:46.701
There's no deal.
It's closed down.


01:06:46.737 --> 01:06:48.069
Good morning.


01:06:48.104 --> 01:06:50.905
<i>But, for me, the last 20 years</i>


01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:53.641
<i>was a very painful experience.</i>


01:06:53.677 --> 01:06:55.142
<i>We're not going to recognize it.</i>


01:06:55.178 --> 01:06:57.412
We don't want to discuss it.


01:06:59.015 --> 01:07:00.247
<i>Painful not only because</i>


01:07:00.283 --> 01:07:03.318
<i>of the lack of
a favorable outcome,</i>


01:07:03.353 --> 01:07:04.786
<i>but also because there was</i>


01:07:05.755 --> 01:07:09.157
no way in to really


01:07:09.192 --> 01:07:11.025
take hold
of the process and say,


01:07:11.060 --> 01:07:13.228
"Okay, let me help you here."


01:07:16.499 --> 01:07:20.235
<i>I was relieved when
I got to Paris because</i>


01:07:20.270 --> 01:07:22.904
<i>there were
influential men and women</i>


01:07:22.939 --> 01:07:25.339
<i>from countries around the world,</i>


01:07:25.374 --> 01:07:28.209
<i>some who'd been through
my training program...</i>


01:07:28.245 --> 01:07:30.878
<i>...and who asked me
to help in the process.</i>


01:07:30.914 --> 01:07:32.113
Thank you.


01:07:35.819 --> 01:07:37.552
- There's the boss.
- Hey!


01:07:37.587 --> 01:07:38.587
How are you, dear?


01:07:39.523 --> 01:07:41.823
- Good to see you.
- Good to see you.


01:07:42.692 --> 01:07:43.825
You ready for me?


01:07:45.295 --> 01:07:49.330
I was in India
earlier this year, and...


01:07:49.365 --> 01:07:51.932
I think that the emergence
of solar electricity


01:07:51.967 --> 01:07:55.136
as a competitive viable option,


01:07:55.171 --> 01:07:58.272
it opens up a pathway


01:07:58.307 --> 01:08:00.942
that justifies realistic hope.


01:08:00.977 --> 01:08:03.077
But, you know,
I do think that India,


01:08:03.113 --> 01:08:04.611
well, Prime Minister Modi,


01:08:04.647 --> 01:08:08.149
has been very, very clear
that their concern is


01:08:08.184 --> 01:08:10.284
not just the existence
of technology,


01:08:10.319 --> 01:08:12.353
but their access
to that technology.


01:08:12.388 --> 01:08:15.656
So... With what trust
do they let go


01:08:15.692 --> 01:08:18.593
of the whole fossil fuel
development model


01:08:18.628 --> 01:08:23.297
that has been paraded in front
of them for 150 years,


01:08:23.332 --> 01:08:24.498
and now we're saying,


01:08:24.534 --> 01:08:26.234
"Excuse me,
this parade, it's done."


01:08:26.269 --> 01:08:28.870
"Now we have to build
a different parade for you."


01:08:28.905 --> 01:08:34.108
It would be most helpful
if that focus on renewables


01:08:34.144 --> 01:08:36.110
can be seen from the developing


01:08:36.146 --> 01:08:37.945
- country's perspective.
- Yeah.


01:08:37.980 --> 01:08:40.548
But is that something
that you could


01:08:40.583 --> 01:08:42.483
devote some time to
while you're here?


01:08:42.519 --> 01:08:43.519
Of course.


01:08:47.857 --> 01:08:49.023
I'm trying to solve


01:08:49.059 --> 01:08:50.825
an Indian problem,
first and foremost.


01:08:51.861 --> 01:08:56.197
Their plan is to build 400


01:08:56.232 --> 01:08:58.466
new dirty coal plants.


01:08:58.501 --> 01:09:00.468
Uh, and that's a disaster.


01:09:01.972 --> 01:09:04.038
When India goes to borrow money


01:09:04.073 --> 01:09:06.507
to build solar farms
or wind farms,


01:09:06.543 --> 01:09:10.678
they have to pay 13%
plus an FX uncertainty.


01:09:11.413 --> 01:09:13.815
And that is manifestly absurd.


01:09:15.584 --> 01:09:16.584
Hey, Ben.


01:09:16.619 --> 01:09:18.886
- Hey, Cory.
- Very good to see you.


01:09:18.921 --> 01:09:21.289
Hey, Ed. How are you?


01:09:21.324 --> 01:09:22.623
<i>The challenge for us</i>


01:09:22.658 --> 01:09:25.759
is to find a way to make
renewables as cheap as coal,


01:09:25.795 --> 01:09:27.095
and then partner with them


01:09:27.130 --> 01:09:29.830
so that we don't build
this huge infrastructure


01:09:29.865 --> 01:09:31.365
that will continue to provide


01:09:31.400 --> 01:09:34.235
more carbon dioxide
decades in the future.


01:09:34.271 --> 01:09:39.974
India has this plan,
200 gigawatts of coal.


01:09:40.009 --> 01:09:41.842
I met with their energy


01:09:41.878 --> 01:09:44.512
and power minister,
Piyush Goyal, in Delhi.


01:09:44.547 --> 01:09:46.648
I asked,
what would it take to shift


01:09:46.683 --> 01:09:48.282
another 100 gigawatts


01:09:48.317 --> 01:09:50.218
from coal to renewables?


01:09:50.253 --> 01:09:52.820
His answer was
incredibly specific.


01:09:53.356 --> 01:09:55.489
Access to credit.


01:09:55.524 --> 01:09:57.536
And, of course, with the
future of human civilization


01:09:57.560 --> 01:09:59.160
in the balance,
we should probably


01:09:59.195 --> 01:10:00.472
- ratchet that up a little bit.
- Wow.


01:10:00.496 --> 01:10:02.229
<i>More than 65%</i>


01:10:02.265 --> 01:10:04.465
<i>of the world's carbon pollution</i>


01:10:04.501 --> 01:10:07.067
<i>comes from the developing world.</i>


01:10:07.103 --> 01:10:08.381
<i>Now, we're not pointing fingers.</i>


01:10:08.405 --> 01:10:10.504
<i>This isn't a question of blame.</i>


01:10:10.539 --> 01:10:12.806
<i>But we're here to change it.</i>


01:10:16.312 --> 01:10:18.379
<i>The reason
that talks are stalled?</i>


01:10:18.414 --> 01:10:20.481
<i>Because the U.S. government</i>


01:10:20.516 --> 01:10:23.317
<i>put countries like India
as culprits,</i>


01:10:23.353 --> 01:10:25.953
as criminals, climate criminals.


01:10:25.988 --> 01:10:27.388
And I don't think the U.S.


01:10:27.423 --> 01:10:29.824
should be allowed
to get away with that.


01:10:32.829 --> 01:10:37.598
Fabius has asked for
an urgent meeting, one on one.


01:10:37.633 --> 01:10:40.000
They're getting frustrated


01:10:40.036 --> 01:10:41.669
at the pace of the negotiations.


01:10:46.342 --> 01:10:47.642
The long-term goal?


01:10:56.886 --> 01:11:00.654
You spend a lot of time
each year in India.


01:11:00.690 --> 01:11:04.826
So my narrow interest
is a way around


01:11:04.861 --> 01:11:06.227
these high interest rates that


01:11:06.262 --> 01:11:08.930
the Indians are
so concerned about


01:11:08.965 --> 01:11:10.364
in deploying solar and wind.


01:11:10.399 --> 01:11:12.200
But we're not gonna
sort that out


01:11:12.235 --> 01:11:13.635
in the next couple of days.


01:11:14.604 --> 01:11:15.604
Yeah.


01:11:20.209 --> 01:11:21.409
<i>The overnight deluge</i>


01:11:21.444 --> 01:11:23.377
<i>of some 300 millimeters of rain</i>


01:11:23.412 --> 01:11:26.614
<i>in a span of 18 hours
has wreaked havoc.</i>


01:11:26.649 --> 01:11:29.150
<i>The news is
that it's only gonna get worse</i>


01:11:29.185 --> 01:11:30.684
<i>and the flood waters are rising.</i>


01:11:30.720 --> 01:11:32.486
<i>India's fourth-largest city</i>


01:11:32.521 --> 01:11:34.055
<i>was paralyzed
by the heaviest rains</i>


01:11:34.090 --> 01:11:35.756
<i>in more than a hundred years,</i>


01:11:35.792 --> 01:11:38.159
<i>which cut off more than
three million people</i>


01:11:38.194 --> 01:11:40.528
<i>from basic services for days.</i>


01:11:49.872 --> 01:11:51.605
It's amazing that
while we're here


01:11:51.641 --> 01:11:54.375
and India is a holdout,
they're having this.


01:12:03.085 --> 01:12:05.452
Part of their thing
is that they need more money


01:12:05.487 --> 01:12:07.020
to deal with it, right?


01:12:07.056 --> 01:12:11.992
So the sticking point is also
on that side in terms of


01:12:12.027 --> 01:12:14.896
developed countries not
wanting to give that money.


01:12:15.898 --> 01:12:17.665
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yes.


01:12:19.435 --> 01:12:21.803
<i>During the days that we are here</i>


01:12:21.838 --> 01:12:24.538
<i>in this conference,</i>


01:12:24.574 --> 01:12:28.909
<i>we have seen a repeat
of the pattern of more floods</i>


01:12:28.944 --> 01:12:30.811
<i>and landslides.</i>


01:12:30.846 --> 01:12:36.550
<i>Indeed today, in Chennai,
these events continue.</i>


01:12:36.586 --> 01:12:40.788
<i>They have had
1,500 millimeters of rain</i>


01:12:40.824 --> 01:12:43.390
in the last several weeks alone.


01:12:43.425 --> 01:12:45.526
Five feet of rain.


01:12:45.561 --> 01:12:47.895
The number of people
who have been killed


01:12:47.931 --> 01:12:49.630
is in the hundreds.


01:12:49.666 --> 01:12:52.567
Tens of thousands
have been relocated.


01:12:53.903 --> 01:12:56.570
This is not the first time
this has happened.


01:12:56.605 --> 01:12:59.406
Every storm is different now


01:12:59.441 --> 01:13:01.909
because of the climate crisis.


01:13:06.649 --> 01:13:12.053
<i>Prime Minister Modi went from
Paris back to Tamil Nadu</i>


01:13:12.088 --> 01:13:15.522
<i>and said, "We are feeling
climate change's"</i>


01:13:15.558 --> 01:13:17.925
"fast-growing impact now."


01:13:29.638 --> 01:13:30.838
<i>No, no, it's all right.</i>


01:13:30.873 --> 01:13:34.108
<i>I'm sorry to call you
on a Saturday evening.</i>


01:13:34.143 --> 01:13:37.011
So, the other sticking point


01:13:37.046 --> 01:13:39.813
is, um, you know, credit


01:13:39.848 --> 01:13:42.650
at a sufficiently
low interest rate


01:13:42.685 --> 01:13:45.019
to allow India to borrow.


01:13:45.722 --> 01:13:47.087
Uh, and...


01:13:47.123 --> 01:13:48.856
But that's a separate issue.


01:13:48.892 --> 01:13:51.959
So, here is the idea that I had.


01:13:53.395 --> 01:13:58.599
Elon has a record
of giving up IP


01:13:58.635 --> 01:14:02.570
on some of the core aspects
in Tesla.


01:14:02.605 --> 01:14:06.540
You know, you take it
and do what you can with it.


01:14:06.576 --> 01:14:09.409
I talked to President Hollande
about it also.


01:14:09.445 --> 01:14:11.278
I didn't mention the company,


01:14:11.314 --> 01:14:13.514
uh, but I mentioned
the general idea.


01:14:13.549 --> 01:14:16.216
Hollande and Ban Ki-moon,


01:14:16.251 --> 01:14:17.451
and I'm sure, at least


01:14:17.486 --> 01:14:19.653
at the ministerial level,
with India,


01:14:19.688 --> 01:14:22.156
we could have, uh, you know,


01:14:22.192 --> 01:14:27.628
halo equity for SolarCity
that would be incredible.


01:14:27.663 --> 01:14:30.263
But before I call
Lyndon or Elon,


01:14:30.299 --> 01:14:32.266
I wanted to run it by you.


01:14:32.301 --> 01:14:33.634
Yeah, would you, please?


01:14:33.670 --> 01:14:36.971
And also send me Lyndon's
cell phone if you have it.


01:14:42.111 --> 01:14:43.444
Lyndon?


01:14:43.479 --> 01:14:45.012
Hi, it's Al Gore.


01:14:45.748 --> 01:14:47.615
I have a big idea for you.


01:14:48.617 --> 01:14:51.685
SolarCity could be


01:14:51.720 --> 01:14:55.590
the corporate hero of Paris


01:14:56.425 --> 01:15:01.696
by announcing that it will give


01:15:02.564 --> 01:15:05.198
the technology
to the new world-champion


01:15:05.234 --> 01:15:06.934
solar cell


01:15:07.369 --> 01:15:08.569
to India.


01:15:12.708 --> 01:15:15.142
Well, here's the logic
for it, Lyndon.


01:15:15.177 --> 01:15:19.680
The big holdout here
in the negotiations is India.


01:15:22.318 --> 01:15:24.285
Yeah, but that's up
to them, okay?


01:15:24.320 --> 01:15:25.586
That's up to them.


01:15:26.555 --> 01:15:28.422
I'm talking about
breaking the impasse


01:15:28.457 --> 01:15:30.024
and getting them over the hump.


01:15:31.394 --> 01:15:33.561
Give it some thought, my friend.


01:15:34.030 --> 01:15:35.096
Bye.


01:15:36.798 --> 01:15:38.532
So he swallowed hard and said,


01:15:38.567 --> 01:15:40.127
"Ugh, I might have
to think about that."


01:15:41.638 --> 01:15:42.837
We'll see.


01:15:49.979 --> 01:15:51.145
<i>It's crunch time</i>


01:15:51.180 --> 01:15:53.380
<i>at the climate change
conference in Paris.</i>


01:15:53.415 --> 01:15:54.781
<i>With a deadline</i>


01:15:54.817 --> 01:15:56.628
<i>for a deal fast approaching,
it's clear that...</i>


01:15:56.652 --> 01:15:57.763
<i>There's still some really tough</i>


01:15:57.787 --> 01:15:59.019
<i>negotiations going on</i>


01:15:59.055 --> 01:16:00.888
<i>among developed
and developing nations.</i>


01:16:00.923 --> 01:16:03.156
There is an argument
about who's responsible


01:16:03.192 --> 01:16:04.425
for climate change,


01:16:04.460 --> 01:16:05.926
who should pay
for the consequences.


01:16:05.961 --> 01:16:07.539
<i>What do you think
of the global warming summit</i>


01:16:07.563 --> 01:16:08.729
<i>in Paris this week?</i>


01:16:08.764 --> 01:16:10.130
<i>I think it's ridiculous.</i>


01:16:10.166 --> 01:16:12.533
<i>We have bigger problems
right now.</i>


01:16:12.568 --> 01:16:14.868
<i>To have the President be there
for an extended</i>


01:16:14.903 --> 01:16:16.503
<i>period of time talking about</i>


01:16:16.539 --> 01:16:18.339
<i>global warming
being the biggest problem</i>


01:16:18.374 --> 01:16:20.941
<i>facing this country is insane.</i>


01:16:20.977 --> 01:16:23.811
<i>He ought to get back to work
and solve the ISIS problem.</i>


01:16:25.548 --> 01:16:28.015
<i>So, Mr. Minister,</i>


01:16:28.050 --> 01:16:31.018
John Kerry asked me
to talk with you


01:16:31.054 --> 01:16:33.888
and explain some of the details


01:16:34.724 --> 01:16:38.692
on this extraordinary gift


01:16:38.727 --> 01:16:40.694
being proposed by, uh,


01:16:40.730 --> 01:16:43.798
the premier solar energy company


01:16:43.833 --> 01:16:45.333
in the United States.


01:16:46.335 --> 01:16:48.235
Give me the, uh, material.


01:16:48.271 --> 01:16:50.071
"It's a hybrid polysilicon..."


01:16:50.672 --> 01:16:52.973
And this is, uh...


01:16:53.009 --> 01:16:58.011
It is a "silicon-based
bifacial PV cell"


01:16:58.047 --> 01:17:01.348
"that combines
n-type substrates,"


01:17:01.384 --> 01:17:03.584
"copper electrodes,"


01:17:03.619 --> 01:17:06.754
"thin-film passivation layers"


01:17:06.789 --> 01:17:09.022
"and a tunneling oxide layer"


01:17:09.057 --> 01:17:12.393
"that yields
high conversion efficiencies."


01:17:13.495 --> 01:17:16.297
And if, in return for this,


01:17:16.933 --> 01:17:19.400
India remove its potential


01:17:19.435 --> 01:17:22.637
objections to
the climate treaty,


01:17:22.672 --> 01:17:27.308
then he said SolarCity
would be willing to do this.


01:17:30.380 --> 01:17:32.813
<i>Is there any precedent
for a technology</i>


01:17:32.848 --> 01:17:35.549
<i>transfer to happen so quickly?</i>


01:17:35.584 --> 01:17:37.250
<i>Well, you all know
about computer chips,</i>


01:17:37.286 --> 01:17:39.753
<i>but let's take
a more mundane example.</i>


01:17:39.788 --> 01:17:41.354
<i>Uh, cell phones.</i>


01:17:41.390 --> 01:17:42.656
<i>Back around 1980,</i>


01:17:42.692 --> 01:17:44.725
I was in
the House of Representatives,


01:17:44.760 --> 01:17:46.059
and I was so excited to buy


01:17:46.094 --> 01:17:49.096
one of the very first
mobile phones.


01:17:49.131 --> 01:17:52.233
Honestly, I felt that thing
was so cool.


01:17:53.535 --> 01:17:55.069
And now there are
more mobile phones


01:17:55.104 --> 01:17:58.072
than there are
people in the world.


01:17:58.107 --> 01:18:00.674
Most of it in
developing countries


01:18:00.709 --> 01:18:03.977
that had no landline
telephone grid.


01:18:04.013 --> 01:18:05.712
And so they could leapfrog


01:18:05.747 --> 01:18:08.349
and get telephone service
for the first time.


01:18:09.285 --> 01:18:10.751
Well, guess what?


01:18:10.786 --> 01:18:12.453
There are a lot of countries


01:18:12.488 --> 01:18:14.588
where the landline
electricity grids


01:18:14.623 --> 01:18:16.523
are not so great.


01:18:16.559 --> 01:18:19.827
In Africa.
In the Indian subcontinent.


01:18:20.596 --> 01:18:22.529
Now we're seeing this instead:


01:18:22.565 --> 01:18:23.864
Solar panels.


01:18:23.899 --> 01:18:26.934
This one in Africa's
on the roof of a grass hut.


01:18:26.969 --> 01:18:29.602
Parents want their children
to have access


01:18:29.638 --> 01:18:31.972
to the universe of information.


01:18:32.007 --> 01:18:35.408
Chile. A developing country,
but it has great policy.


01:18:35.444 --> 01:18:38.479
You talk about excitement,
this story gets me excited.


01:18:38.514 --> 01:18:42.249
At the end of 2013, they had
11 megawatts of solar.


01:18:42.284 --> 01:18:46.219
By the end of 2014,
more than 400 megawatts.


01:18:46.255 --> 01:18:49.022
By the end of last year,
more than 800 megawatts.


01:18:49.058 --> 01:18:52.859
So look at what they have
under construction this year


01:18:52.894 --> 01:18:56.063
and under contract to
soon begin construction.


01:19:08.611 --> 01:19:10.478
Isn't that great? I love this.


01:19:12.348 --> 01:19:15.048
13.3 gigawatts!


01:19:15.084 --> 01:19:18.986
And there are other countries
and many regions in the world


01:19:19.021 --> 01:19:23.123
<i>that are poised
for this kind of breakout.</i>


01:19:23.159 --> 01:19:26.426
<i>We are seeing
a real turning here.</i>


01:19:26.462 --> 01:19:27.928
<i>On a global basis,</i>


01:19:27.964 --> 01:19:32.899
the world gets more energy
from the sun each hour


01:19:32.934 --> 01:19:35.369
than the entire global economy


01:19:35.404 --> 01:19:37.872
uses for an entire year.


01:19:37.907 --> 01:19:40.574
If we increase
the fraction of that


01:19:40.610 --> 01:19:43.477
that we harvest
and use productively,


01:19:43.512 --> 01:19:47.448
then we can solve that part
of the climate crisis.


01:19:51.888 --> 01:19:53.320
<i>There's been a lot of discussion</i>


01:19:53.355 --> 01:19:54.755
<i>between India and U.S.</i>


01:19:54.790 --> 01:19:56.322
<i>We know that President Obama</i>


01:19:56.358 --> 01:19:59.660
<i>did make a call
to Prime Minister Modi.</i>


01:19:59.695 --> 01:20:01.695
<i>What the outcomes
were of those talks,</i>


01:20:01.730 --> 01:20:03.430
<i>we don't know yet.</i>


01:20:03.465 --> 01:20:04.798
<i>What we do know is John Kerry</i>


01:20:04.833 --> 01:20:06.233
has been having
repeated discussion.


01:20:06.268 --> 01:20:07.934
There was
a one-hour meeting today


01:20:07.969 --> 01:20:10.137
with the environment minister.


01:20:10.172 --> 01:20:12.840
<i>How all this translates
into India's concerns</i>


01:20:12.875 --> 01:20:15.176
<i>being met
still remains to be seen.</i>


01:20:16.144 --> 01:20:17.444
Do we have a deal,
Mr. President?


01:20:17.479 --> 01:20:18.546
Do we have a deal?


01:20:43.105 --> 01:20:45.072
Today is a historic day.


01:20:46.008 --> 01:20:48.675
We have,
in the spirit of compromise,


01:20:48.710 --> 01:20:51.045
agreed on a number of phrases.


01:20:51.580 --> 01:20:54.715
And we have not only


01:20:54.750 --> 01:20:57.384
an agreement,
but we have written


01:20:57.419 --> 01:20:59.286
a new chapter of hope


01:20:59.321 --> 01:21:02.256
in the lives of seven billion
people on the planet.


01:21:02.825 --> 01:21:04.891
We hope


01:21:04.926 --> 01:21:08.762
that Paris will reassure
this future generation


01:21:08.797 --> 01:21:11.999
that we all together
will mitigate


01:21:12.034 --> 01:21:14.668
the challenge
posed by climate change,


01:21:14.704 --> 01:21:18.038
and we will give them
a better Earth.


01:21:18.807 --> 01:21:20.975
Thank you.<i> Merci beaucoup.</i>


01:21:22.110 --> 01:21:24.844
<i>L'accord de Paris pour
le climat est accepté.</i>


01:21:38.828 --> 01:21:41.161
<i>We started
this COP commemorating</i>


01:21:41.196 --> 01:21:44.064
<i>those fallen in tragic deaths.</i>


01:21:44.099 --> 01:21:47.167
<i>We end this COP celebrating</i>


01:21:47.202 --> 01:21:49.970
a new chapter of hope
for the world.


01:21:58.213 --> 01:22:00.080
This is exciting.
It's very exciting.


01:22:02.551 --> 01:22:04.751
Yes, thank you, sir.


01:22:04.787 --> 01:22:06.153
Thank you. I'm glad it helped.


01:22:26.308 --> 01:22:28.041
<i>The Paris Agreement
would not have happened</i>


01:22:28.077 --> 01:22:30.244
<i>without President Obama,</i>


01:22:30.279 --> 01:22:32.579
<i>Secretary of State John Kerry,</i>


01:22:32.614 --> 01:22:35.215
<i>President Hollande of France.</i>


01:22:35.250 --> 01:22:37.952
<i>Without a lot of people
who worked hard on that.</i>


01:22:39.288 --> 01:22:43.289
Virtually every nation
in the entire world agreed


01:22:43.325 --> 01:22:46.760
to get to net-zero
greenhouse gas emissions


01:22:46.795 --> 01:22:50.631
as early in the second half
of this century as possible.


01:22:50.666 --> 01:22:52.766
This sends a signal to markets,


01:22:52.801 --> 01:22:54.668
sends a signal to investors.


01:22:54.703 --> 01:22:56.036
And it resulted


01:22:56.072 --> 01:23:00.140
in the largest solar loan
in the history of the world


01:23:00.176 --> 01:23:02.943
to help India move forward
more quickly


01:23:02.978 --> 01:23:05.312
in its installation of solar.


01:23:06.014 --> 01:23:07.580
It needs continuing work.


01:23:07.616 --> 01:23:10.917
And one of the reasons
your role is so important


01:23:10.952 --> 01:23:13.620
is to hold them to
what they've agreed to


01:23:13.655 --> 01:23:15.522
and to keep the pressure on.


01:23:15.557 --> 01:23:17.858
Because even with
all the agreements


01:23:17.893 --> 01:23:20.226
put together,
it's still not enough.


01:23:20.262 --> 01:23:23.029
But the basis is there
to ramp up


01:23:23.064 --> 01:23:25.432
and make more progress.


01:23:25.467 --> 01:23:28.635
There are cities in the U.S.
that have already


01:23:28.670 --> 01:23:32.539
reached the goal of
100% renewable energy.


01:23:32.574 --> 01:23:35.975
Rock Port, Missouri.
Greensburg, Kansas.


01:23:36.011 --> 01:23:38.178
They were destroyed
by an F5 tornado,


01:23:38.214 --> 01:23:40.513
and when they built back,
they say, "Hey",


01:23:40.549 --> 01:23:43.450
"let's go 100% renewable,"
and they have.


01:23:46.522 --> 01:23:47.487
<i>Right here in Houston,</i>


01:23:47.523 --> 01:23:52.058
<i>the home of the global
oil and gas industry,</i>


01:23:52.094 --> 01:23:53.961
<i>you'll hear a lot of people say,</i>


01:23:53.996 --> 01:23:55.128
<i>"Wind and solar's nice,"</i>


01:23:55.163 --> 01:23:57.865
<i>"but it won't really
lead anywhere."</i>


01:23:57.900 --> 01:23:59.332
And a lot of people
are now saying


01:23:59.367 --> 01:24:04.504
that is a strange new form
of denial.


01:24:04.539 --> 01:24:09.043
<i>But look at what's happening
in Georgetown, Texas.</i>


01:24:26.795 --> 01:24:27.994
Howdy!


01:24:28.030 --> 01:24:29.173
Howdy, Mr. Vice President Gore.


01:24:29.197 --> 01:24:30.363
How are you, Mr. Mayor?


01:24:30.399 --> 01:24:33.066
Welcome to the greatest city
on Planet Earth.


01:24:33.101 --> 01:24:35.235
Spoken like a good mayor.


01:24:37.006 --> 01:24:38.638
- Can I have one?
- Oh, sure.


01:24:38.673 --> 01:24:40.107
I know they're for kids.


01:24:40.142 --> 01:24:41.419
So, how long have you
been mayor?


01:24:41.443 --> 01:24:43.176
I have been mayor for two years.


01:24:43.211 --> 01:24:45.712
This green energy
initiative started


01:24:45.747 --> 01:24:47.881
when I was on council in 2008.


01:24:47.916 --> 01:24:48.982
I remember that. Mmm-hmm.


01:24:49.018 --> 01:24:50.917
And the direction that
we gave to Chris


01:24:50.952 --> 01:24:53.486
and crew was, in 2008, we said


01:24:53.521 --> 01:24:56.456
by 2030 we want
our energy portfolio


01:24:56.491 --> 01:24:59.092
- to be 30% renewable energy.
- Yeah.


01:24:59.128 --> 01:25:01.028
Well, they've sort of
blown by that.


01:25:01.063 --> 01:25:02.662
That was ambitious
at that time, anyway.


01:25:02.698 --> 01:25:04.197
It was! Absolutely.


01:25:04.232 --> 01:25:06.099
Currently we're
90% renewable energy.


01:25:06.134 --> 01:25:08.101
We're getting our wind
out of Amarillo.


01:25:08.136 --> 01:25:10.437
Our solar farm's being
built out in west Texas.


01:25:10.472 --> 01:25:12.872
It should be up and running
next spring.


01:25:12.907 --> 01:25:14.107
Oh.


01:25:14.142 --> 01:25:17.210
And currently, we will be
the first city in Texas


01:25:17.245 --> 01:25:18.945
to be 100% renewable.


01:25:18.980 --> 01:25:20.714
And when we go 100% renewable,


01:25:20.749 --> 01:25:23.150
we will be the largest city
in the country


01:25:23.185 --> 01:25:25.518
that's renewable energy at 100%.


01:25:25.553 --> 01:25:29.356
So, I assume that
the reason you did this


01:25:29.391 --> 01:25:32.693
is that the two of you are
just rabid environmentalists.


01:25:32.728 --> 01:25:34.127
Well, not exactly, because,


01:25:34.163 --> 01:25:36.363
um, you're in Georgetown,


01:25:36.398 --> 01:25:38.264
which is the reddest city


01:25:38.300 --> 01:25:40.400
in the reddest county in Texas,


01:25:40.435 --> 01:25:42.368
and I'm a conservative
Republican.


01:25:42.404 --> 01:25:43.636
- Yeah.
- Okay.


01:25:43.672 --> 01:25:47.174
But our duty to our ratepayers
is to provide them


01:25:47.209 --> 01:25:49.642
with the lowest possible
utility cost.


01:25:49.678 --> 01:25:51.111
And money talks.


01:25:51.146 --> 01:25:52.490
But then, wouldn't it
just make sense


01:25:52.514 --> 01:25:53.914
from a common-sense standpoint,


01:25:53.949 --> 01:25:57.150
the less stuff you put
in the air, the better it is?


01:25:57.186 --> 01:25:59.052
- Yeah.
- I mean, common sense.


01:25:59.087 --> 01:26:00.754
You don't need scientists
to debate that.


01:26:00.789 --> 01:26:02.589
- Can I use that line?
- You absolutely can.


01:26:09.431 --> 01:26:10.597
<i>Hey, let me tell you what.</i>


01:26:10.632 --> 01:26:13.533
<i>You would be
the sport of all sports</i>


01:26:13.569 --> 01:26:16.302
<i>if somebody could get
a picture of me</i>


01:26:16.338 --> 01:26:18.471
<i>and Mr. Vice President?</i>


01:26:18.507 --> 01:26:19.884
That's just for
personal use, right?


01:26:19.908 --> 01:26:21.275
Oh, yeah.


01:26:22.644 --> 01:26:25.379
- All right, here we go.
- Come on.


01:26:25.414 --> 01:26:27.247
Here's your camera right there.


01:26:28.617 --> 01:26:30.283
Now, this is not an endorsement,


01:26:30.318 --> 01:26:31.852
as you can well imagine.


01:26:37.325 --> 01:26:38.758
Hi, how are you?


01:26:38.794 --> 01:26:40.860
Hi, Beth Wade,
<i>Community Impact</i> newspaper.


01:26:40.896 --> 01:26:42.129
Pleasure. Pleasure.


01:26:42.164 --> 01:26:43.897
You think this city
can be a trailblazer


01:26:43.932 --> 01:26:46.066
for other cities of like size?


01:26:46.101 --> 01:26:47.267
Definitely.


01:26:47.302 --> 01:26:49.669
Now, not every city has
a CPA as a mayor,


01:26:49.705 --> 01:26:51.704
so that's another
advantage for you.


01:26:52.741 --> 01:26:56.142
But when other cities
really look at the facts,


01:26:56.177 --> 01:26:59.212
I think you're gonna see
a massive wave


01:26:59.247 --> 01:27:00.913
switching over to


01:27:00.949 --> 01:27:03.116
pollution-free renewable energy.


01:27:03.151 --> 01:27:04.851
Well, what we always say is,


01:27:04.886 --> 01:27:06.720
don't we have a moral
and ethical obligation


01:27:06.755 --> 01:27:09.356
to leave the planet
better than we found it?


01:27:09.391 --> 01:27:11.324
You better be careful
talking like that.


01:27:17.566 --> 01:27:20.534
<i>T-minus 10, 9, 8,</i>


01:27:20.903 --> 01:27:23.736
<i>7, 6, 5,</i>


01:27:23.772 --> 01:27:27.007
<i>4, 3, 2, 1,</i>


01:27:27.543 --> 01:27:28.842
<i>0.</i>


01:27:28.877 --> 01:27:30.877
<i>Liftoff of the Falcon 9.</i>


01:27:37.185 --> 01:27:39.286
<i>Falcon 9 has cleared the towers.</i>


01:27:43.158 --> 01:27:45.559
<i>The Falcon takes flight,</i>


01:27:45.594 --> 01:27:49.129
<i>propelling the Deep Space
Climate Observatory DSCOVR</i>


01:27:49.164 --> 01:27:53.500
<i>on a million-mile journey
to protect our Planet Earth.</i>


01:28:03.411 --> 01:28:05.145
<i>When President Obama
was elected,</i>


01:28:05.180 --> 01:28:07.447
<i>I went to see him and</i>


01:28:07.482 --> 01:28:11.017
<i>told him the story of
the DSCOVR satellite and</i>


01:28:11.052 --> 01:28:13.320
<i>how it was cancelled.</i>


01:28:15.657 --> 01:28:20.327
And he allocated the money,
and it finally got launched.


01:28:21.296 --> 01:28:22.863
<i>Second stage engine</i>


01:28:23.932 --> 01:28:25.332
<i>ignition</i>


01:28:26.668 --> 01:28:28.335
<i>has occurred on time.</i>


01:28:34.509 --> 01:28:37.577
<i>Remember, when I
mentioned the Blue Marble,</i>


01:28:37.612 --> 01:28:41.615
<i>it was the only photograph
of its kind.</i>


01:28:42.550 --> 01:28:44.751
<i>But here's one
from the first week</i>


01:28:44.787 --> 01:28:46.820
<i>of last December.</i>


01:28:48.056 --> 01:28:50.390
<i>This was taken by a brand-new</i>


01:28:50.425 --> 01:28:55.261
<i>NASA and NOAA satellite
called DSCOVR</i>


01:28:55.297 --> 01:28:58.665
<i>that orbits the sun
along with the Earth</i>


01:28:58.700 --> 01:29:01.468
and the scientists
are learning so much.


01:29:01.503 --> 01:29:03.035
For example, for the first time


01:29:03.071 --> 01:29:04.270
they're learning about


01:29:04.306 --> 01:29:07.207
the real energy balance
of the Earth.


01:29:07.242 --> 01:29:10.343
Energy in and energy out.


01:29:10.378 --> 01:29:11.945
<i>Don't let anybody tell you</i>


01:29:11.980 --> 01:29:14.580
<i>that we're gonna get on
rocket ships and go to</i>


01:29:14.616 --> 01:29:17.517
<i>Mars and live in hermetically
sealed buildings.</i>


01:29:17.552 --> 01:29:19.586
<i>We couldn't even evacuate
the city of New Orleans</i>


01:29:19.621 --> 01:29:22.288
<i>when the hurricane hit there.</i>


01:29:22.324 --> 01:29:23.990
<i>This is our home.</i>


01:29:38.640 --> 01:29:40.406
<i>Delegates at the United Nations</i>


01:29:40.442 --> 01:29:42.242
<i>Climate Summit
are expressing panic</i>


01:29:42.277 --> 01:29:44.377
<i>over Tuesday's election results,</i>


01:29:44.412 --> 01:29:45.946
<i>saying President-elect
Donald Trump</i>


01:29:45.981 --> 01:29:49.549
<i>may threaten the future
of any international agreement</i>


01:29:49.584 --> 01:29:51.818
<i>to slow catastrophic
climate change.</i>


01:29:51.854 --> 01:29:55.822
<i>Trump has said he will "cancel
the Paris Climate Agreement"</i>


01:29:55.857 --> 01:29:59.392
<i>and also promised to promote
coal power and fracking,</i>


01:29:59.427 --> 01:30:00.593
<i>and says he will allow</i>


01:30:00.629 --> 01:30:02.429
<i>for oil and gas drilling
on federal land.</i>


01:30:02.464 --> 01:30:03.864
<i>A famous boxer once said,</i>


01:30:03.899 --> 01:30:05.732
<i>"Everybody has a plan"</i>


01:30:05.767 --> 01:30:07.968
<i>"until he gets
punched in the face."</i>


01:30:10.038 --> 01:30:11.671
<i>Days into his presidency,</i>


01:30:11.706 --> 01:30:14.574
<i>Donald Trump is sending
chills down the spines</i>


01:30:14.610 --> 01:30:17.877
<i>of environmentalists
and some EPA employees.</i>


01:30:17.912 --> 01:30:20.280
<i>This trifecta
of cabinet appointments,</i>


01:30:20.315 --> 01:30:23.149
<i>all staunch
fossil fuel supporters</i>


01:30:23.184 --> 01:30:24.417
<i>who've expressed doubts</i>


01:30:24.452 --> 01:30:27.887
<i>about the urgency
of climate change.</i>


01:30:27.922 --> 01:30:29.722
<i>For all the years
I've been involved</i>


01:30:29.758 --> 01:30:32.893
<i>in this struggle, there have
been lots of setbacks.</i>


01:30:34.729 --> 01:30:36.496
<i>So now we have another one.</i>


01:30:44.172 --> 01:30:45.738
<i>With all these new threats,</i>


01:30:45.773 --> 01:30:49.108
<i>there's never been
a more important time</i>


01:30:49.144 --> 01:30:51.711
<i>to speak truth to power.</i>


01:30:58.853 --> 01:31:00.153
I do my best


01:31:02.023 --> 01:31:03.990
to speak for


01:31:05.093 --> 01:31:10.796
the public interest in solving
the climate crisis.


01:31:10.832 --> 01:31:14.567
Even though it sounds
a little highfalutin,


01:31:14.603 --> 01:31:19.873
I try to answer to the truth
of what needs to be done.


01:31:20.575 --> 01:31:24.010
And each of us, in our own ways,


01:31:25.981 --> 01:31:30.583
has the obligation
and some ability


01:31:30.619 --> 01:31:34.687
to feel what is more likely
to be true than not.


01:31:34.722 --> 01:31:35.955
And if you work hard enough


01:31:35.990 --> 01:31:38.925
to get the best
available evidence,


01:31:38.961 --> 01:31:43.997
you can feel if you're onto
what the right thing is.


01:31:44.032 --> 01:31:48.401
And that's not arrogance,
that's just a feeling that


01:31:48.437 --> 01:31:51.470
I think everybody
is familiar with.


01:31:51.506 --> 01:31:53.239
And I've been
working on this issue


01:31:53.274 --> 01:31:55.474
long enough that I feel very,


01:31:55.510 --> 01:31:58.511
very deeply about
what the right thing is.


01:31:58.546 --> 01:32:00.514
I'm not confused about it.


01:32:08.256 --> 01:32:10.023
<i>I remember vividly</i>


01:32:10.058 --> 01:32:14.093
<i>when the Civil Rights
Movement first began to</i>


01:32:14.129 --> 01:32:15.896
<i>pick up steam.</i>


01:32:17.499 --> 01:32:20.333
<i>We saw Bull Connor
turning fire hoses</i>


01:32:20.368 --> 01:32:23.503
<i>on young African-American kids,</i>


01:32:23.538 --> 01:32:25.471
<i>and we asked
the older generation</i>


01:32:25.507 --> 01:32:28.108
<i>why it's just and fair
to have laws</i>


01:32:28.143 --> 01:32:30.076
<i>that discriminate
on the basis of skin color.</i>


01:32:30.112 --> 01:32:31.722
<i>And when they couldn't
answer that question,</i>


01:32:31.746 --> 01:32:33.813
<i>the laws began to change.</i>


01:32:35.517 --> 01:32:39.819
<i>This movement
to solve the climate crisis</i>


01:32:39.855 --> 01:32:44.724
is in the tradition of every
great moral movement


01:32:44.759 --> 01:32:47.594
that has advanced
the cause of humankind.


01:32:47.629 --> 01:32:50.396
And every single one of them


01:32:50.431 --> 01:32:53.633
has met with resistance
to the point


01:32:53.668 --> 01:32:56.936
where many of the advocates
felt despair


01:32:56.972 --> 01:33:00.139
and wondered, "How long
is this gonna take?"


01:33:00.175 --> 01:33:02.909
Martin Luther King famously
answered a question during


01:33:02.944 --> 01:33:05.178
some of the bleakest hours
of the Civil Rights Movement,


01:33:05.213 --> 01:33:08.247
when someone asked,
"How long is this gonna take?"


01:33:08.282 --> 01:33:10.450
He said, "How long? Not long."


01:33:10.485 --> 01:33:12.385
"Because no lie
can live forever."


01:33:12.421 --> 01:33:14.487
"How long? Not long."


01:33:14.522 --> 01:33:16.889
"Because the moral arc
of the universe is long,"


01:33:16.924 --> 01:33:18.558
"but it bends toward justice."


01:33:18.593 --> 01:33:19.859
"How long? Not long."


01:33:19.894 --> 01:33:22.628
We are close in this movement.


01:33:22.664 --> 01:33:26.099
We are very close
to the tipping point


01:33:26.134 --> 01:33:29.802
beyond which this movement,
like the abolition movement,


01:33:29.838 --> 01:33:31.637
like the women's
suffrage movement,


01:33:31.673 --> 01:33:33.440
like the Civil Rights Movement,


01:33:33.475 --> 01:33:35.308
like the anti-apartheid
movement,


01:33:35.343 --> 01:33:37.010
like the movement
for gay rights,


01:33:37.045 --> 01:33:41.046
is resolved into a choice
between right and wrong.


01:33:41.082 --> 01:33:43.550
And because of who we are
as human beings,


01:33:43.585 --> 01:33:46.151
the outcome is foreordained.


01:33:46.187 --> 01:33:49.689
And it is right to save
the future for humanity!


01:33:49.724 --> 01:33:52.024
It is wrong
to pollute this Earth


01:33:52.060 --> 01:33:54.694
and destroy the climate balance!


01:33:54.729 --> 01:33:57.764
It is right to give hope
to the future generation!


01:34:00.902 --> 01:34:03.069
<i>It will not be easy.</i>


01:34:03.838 --> 01:34:06.172
<i>And we, too, in this movement</i>


01:34:06.208 --> 01:34:10.876
<i>will encounter a series of no's.</i>


01:34:10.912 --> 01:34:12.879
<i>The great American poet
Wallace Stevens,</i>


01:34:12.914 --> 01:34:15.215
<i>in the last century,</i>


01:34:15.250 --> 01:34:16.849
<i>one of his lines was this,</i>


01:34:16.884 --> 01:34:21.254
<i>"After the last no comes a yes."</i>


01:34:21.289 --> 01:34:25.125
<i>"And on that yes
the future world depends."</i>


01:34:25.760 --> 01:34:27.794
Bye-bye, guys. Thank you!

